	<Folder>
		<name>A. Production</name>
		<Folder>
			<name>Oil Fields</name>
			<Folder>
				<name>Onshore fields</name>
				<Placemark>
					<name>Romanian Onshore Oil Fields (OMV Petrom)</name>
					<description>Status: Operational
Operator: OMV Petrom across hundreds of mature fields — the Videle, Muntenia and Oltenia clusters and the Suplacu de Barcău heavy-oil steamflood among them
Role: Europe&apos;s oldest oil province still at work — Romania recorded the world&apos;s first official crude production statistics in 1857 and lit Bucharest with kerosene the same year
Capacity: ~45,000–50,000 b/d of liquids from a province producing for 165+ years — deep decline managed well by EOR and dense infrastructure
Connectivity: Gathering into Petrobrazi, the refinery built to run exactly this barrel
Strategic role: The historical bedrock of the map — where the industry&apos;s story starts — and the reason Romania retains an integrated national oil company at all
Risks: Terminal decline; abandonment liabilities across thousands of old wells; costs of operating antiquity</description>
					<styleUrl>#msn_target</styleUrl>
					<Point>
						<coordinates>25.52,44.28,0</coordinates>
					</Point>
				</Placemark>
				<Placemark>
					<name>Permian Basin – Midland Basin</name>
					<description>Status: Operational
Operator: Fragmented by design — ExxonMobil (Pioneer), Chevron, Occidental, Diamondback and hundreds of independents
Role: The eastern lobe of the world&apos;s most productive oil basin — the original &quot;West Texas Intermediate&quot; country around Midland-Odessa; stacked shale benches (Spraberry, Wolfcamp) drilled by the thousand
Capacity: The Permian as a whole produces ~6+ million b/d of crude — more than any country except the US itself, Saudi Arabia and Russia; Midland Basin light sweet is the core of the WTI deliverable stream
Connectivity: The Basin pipeline to Cushing (on this map) — the historic route; today most barrels flow straight to Corpus Christi and Houston for export
Recent: The 2026 price spike above $90 tested shale&apos;s defining trait — the only production on Earth that responds to price in months, not years
Risks: Decline-rate treadmill; gas takeaway and flaring constraints; inventory-depth debates; service-cost inflation</description>
					<styleUrl>#msn_target0</styleUrl>
					<Point>
						<coordinates>-102,31.9,0</coordinates>
					</Point>
				</Placemark>
				<Placemark>
					<name>Permian Basin – Delaware Basin</name>
					<description>Status: Operational
Operator: ExxonMobil, Chevron, Occidental, ConocoPhillips, EOG and independents across Texas and New Mexico
Role: The deeper, gassier western lobe of the Permian and its growth engine — the most active drilling zone in the world, straddling the Texas–New Mexico line
Capacity: The Permian&apos;s incremental barrels have come disproportionately from here; New Mexico became the second-largest producing US state on Delaware output alone
Connectivity: Gathering into the Permian trunk systems — toward Cushing via Basin, toward the Gulf via the Corpus Christi and Houston pipelines that now carry the marginal barrel
Recent: Consolidation wave — the majors bought the basin (Pioneer, CrownRock, Endeavor deals) betting decades of inventory
Risks: Water handling at enormous scale; federal-land permitting on the New Mexico side; productivity plateau questions</description>
					<styleUrl>#msn_target0</styleUrl>
					<Point>
						<coordinates>-103.5,31.7,0</coordinates>
					</Point>
				</Placemark>
				<Placemark>
					<name>SCOOP/STACK (Anadarko Basin, Oklahoma)</name>
					<description>Status: Operational
Operator: Continental Resources, Devon, Marathon and Oklahoma independents
Role: The hub&apos;s home-state supply — the shale plays of the Anadarko Basin west of Oklahoma City, a short pipe from Cushing&apos;s tanks
Capacity: Several hundred thousand b/d of light sweet crude and condensate at plateau — modest beside the Permian, but the closest barrels to the benchmark
Connectivity: Local gathering into the Cushing hub — no long-haul economics needed
Strategic role: The reason Oklahoma still produces the barrel it prices — and the marginal local supply that shows up first in the hub&apos;s weekly inventory number
Risks: Play maturity after the 2010s boom; gas-weighted economics; competition for capital against the Permian</description>
					<styleUrl>#msn_target0</styleUrl>
					<Point>
						<coordinates>-97.89999999999999,35.4,0</coordinates>
					</Point>
				</Placemark>
				<Placemark>
					<name>DJ Basin (Denver-Julesburg, Colorado)</name>
					<description>Status: Operational
Operator: Chevron (dominant after the Noble and PDC acquisitions), Occidental, Civitas
Role: Colorado&apos;s shale engine on the Front Range — Niobrara light sweet crude from Weld County, one of the classic inbound streams to Cushing
Capacity: ~400,000+ b/d at plateau
Connectivity: The White Cliffs and Saddlehorn pipelines carry DJ barrels southeast to the Cushing hub — the northern spoke of the crossroads
Strategic role: A steady non-Permian feed that diversifies the deliverable stream — and a regulatory bellwether: Colorado&apos;s setback and permitting politics preview the US onshore&apos;s constraints
Risks: State regulatory tightening; urban-interface drilling limits; single-basin consolidation under Chevron</description>
					<styleUrl>#msn_target0</styleUrl>
					<Point>
						<coordinates>-104.6,40.3,0</coordinates>
					</Point>
				</Placemark>
				<Placemark>
					<name>Bakken (Williston Basin, North Dakota)</name>
					<description>Status: Operational
Operator: ConocoPhillips, Continental, Hess (Chevron), Devon and others
Role: The play that started the shale revolution at scale — light sweet crude from the Williston Basin, ~1 million b/d at plateau
Capacity: ~1 million b/d; the northernmost major feed of the US light sweet system
Connectivity: Mostly southeast via the Dakota Access Pipeline to Patoka and on to Nederland — but Rockies routes (Pony Express toward Cushing) and rail keep it tied to the hub&apos;s orbit
Recent: A decade past peak growth, the Bakken settled into harvest mode — stable output, capital discipline, and the pipeline-politics scars of the DAPL fight
Strategic role: Proof of concept for everything the Permian scaled up — and a swing contributor to the light sweet pool that defines WTI quality
Risks: Winter operations; DAPL legal exposure; basin maturity; rail-dependence in disruptions</description>
					<styleUrl>#msn_target0</styleUrl>
					<Point>
						<coordinates>-103.3,48.10000000000001,0</coordinates>
					</Point>
				</Placemark>
				<Placemark>
					<name>Fahud Oil Field</name>
					<description>Status: Operational
Operator: Petroleum Development Oman (PDO — Omani state 60%, Shell 34%, TotalEnergies 4%, Partex 2%)
Role: One of Oman&apos;s founding fields — the 1964 discovery that launched the industry; northern cluster anchor and gathering node of the interior production system
Capacity: A core contributor within PDO&apos;s ~650,000+ b/d; Omani crude blends light and medium streams from dozens of interior fields
Connectivity: Head of the Main Oil Line over the Hajar mountains to Mina al-Fahal
Recent: PDO sustains output through enhanced recovery across its mature northern fields; Oman held its OPEC+ position through the 2026 crisis with exports unimpeded — no Hormuz transit required
Risks: Field maturity and decline management; interior logistics; single trunkline evacuation</description>
					<styleUrl>#msn_target0</styleUrl>
					<Point>
						<coordinates>56.48,22.35,0</coordinates>
					</Point>
				</Placemark>
				<Placemark>
					<name>Yibal Oil Field</name>
					<description>Status: Operational
Operator: Petroleum Development Oman
Role: Historically PDO&apos;s largest producer — the giant of the northern cluster, a textbook case of peak, steep decline and technology-driven rehabilitation (horizontal wells, water management, the Yibal Khuff deep gas-oil project)
Capacity: Well below its 1990s peak but sustained by redevelopment; the Khuff project added sour gas and condensate processing
Connectivity: Gathering into the Fahud-area system and the Main Oil Line to the coast
Recent: The Yibal Khuff integrated project (onstream 2020s) extended the hub&apos;s life by decades
Risks: Advanced maturity; sour-service complexity at Khuff; decline-rate economics</description>
					<styleUrl>#msn_target0</styleUrl>
					<Point>
						<coordinates>56.03,22.19,0</coordinates>
					</Point>
				</Placemark>
				<Placemark>
					<name>Marmul Oil Field</name>
					<description>Status: Operational
Operator: Petroleum Development Oman
Role: The anchor of PDO&apos;s southern heavy-oil cluster — viscous crude produced with polymer flooding and enhanced recovery since the 1980s; one of the earliest large-scale polymer floods anywhere
Capacity: A major contributor to the southern system&apos;s heavy streams
Connectivity: Southern gathering north toward the Main Oil Line system and the coastal blend
Recent: Solar steam and EOR innovation (the nearby Miraah solar-thermal plant serves PDO&apos;s southern heavy-oil operations) — Oman as a test bed for lower-carbon heavy-oil production
Risks: Heavy-oil economics; EOR energy intensity; long-distance evacuation from the south</description>
					<styleUrl>#msn_target0</styleUrl>
					<Point>
						<coordinates>55.18000000000001,18.14,0</coordinates>
					</Point>
				</Placemark>
				<Placemark>
					<name>Mukhaizna Oil Field</name>
					<description>Status: Operational
Operator: Occidental Petroleum (Oxy Oman) — the largest non-PDO producer
Role: Oman&apos;s flagship foreign-operated heavy-oil project — a massive steam flood in the central desert that multiplied output roughly tenfold after Oxy took over in 2005
Capacity: ~100,000–120,000 b/d of viscous crude at plateau
Connectivity: Evacuation into the national crude system toward the coastal export blend
Recent: Decades-long steam-flood management with produced-water treatment at scale; Oxy&apos;s largest international operation
Risks: Steam energy intensity and gas supply; water handling; heavy-grade pricing</description>
					<styleUrl>#msn_target0</styleUrl>
					<Point>
						<coordinates>56.40000000000001,19.4,0</coordinates>
					</Point>
				</Placemark>
				<Placemark>
					<name>Zubair Oil Field</name>
					<description>Status: Reduced
Operator: Basra Oil Company with an Eni-led consortium as technical service contractor
Role: One of the four southern supergiants — southwest of Basra city, producing since the 1950s; a pillar of the Basrah Medium and Heavy export streams
Capacity: ~400,000 b/d achieved against a renegotiated plateau (originally contracted far higher in the 2009 licensing round)
Connectivity: Degassing and gathering into the southern network, Zubair/Fao storage and the Gulf terminals
Recent: 2026 — throttled with the whole southern system as the Hormuz closure saturated storage and forced shut-ins; Eni remains the last Western supermajor operating a southern Iraqi giant
Risks: Hormuz-dependent evacuation; storage-saturation shut-ins; contractor-economics friction; urban encroachment from Basra</description>
					<styleUrl>#msn_target0</styleUrl>
					<Point>
						<coordinates>47.65594324583399,30.35116389253891,0</coordinates>
					</Point>
				</Placemark>
				<Placemark>
					<name>Majnoon Oil Field</name>
					<description>Status: Reduced
Operator: Basra Oil Company, self-operated since Shell and Petronas exited in 2018
Role: The supergiant whose name means &quot;crazy&quot; — for the size of the reserves found in the marshes northeast of Basra, straddling the geological structure shared with Iran&apos;s Azadegan across the border
Capacity: ~240,000 b/d, with long-deferred expansion ambitions well above that
Connectivity: Central processing into the southern pipeline network toward Fao and the Gulf terminals
Recent: 2026 — cut back with the rest of the south under the Hormuz closure; the field remains the test case of Iraqi self-operatorship after the supermajor exit
Risks: Hormuz-dependent evacuation; border-zone location opposite Iranian Azadegan; marshland environment; capital constraints without a foreign operator</description>
					<styleUrl>#msn_target0</styleUrl>
					<Point>
						<coordinates>47.61137539505276,31.07147118772586,0</coordinates>
					</Point>
				</Placemark>
				<Placemark>
					<name>Bu Hasa Oil Field</name>
					<description>Status: Operational
Operator: ADNOC Onshore
Role: ADNOC&apos;s largest onshore producer — the volume heart of the Murban crude stream, southwest of Abu Dhabi near the Habshan complex; producing since the 1960s
Capacity: ~650,000 b/d after the Bu Hasa integrated-facilities expansion — the biggest single contributor to the UAE&apos;s onshore system
Connectivity: Gathering into Habshan for processing, then the Murban export system — Jebel Dhanna by pipeline west, or ADCOP east to Fujairah beyond the strait
Recent: 2026 — the field behind the UAE&apos;s resilience: Bu Hasa barrels reached market through the Hormuz closure via the Fujairah route while offshore grades sat constrained
Risks: Processing concentration at Habshan; maturity management at high sustained rates; drone exposure of desert facilities</description>
					<styleUrl>#msn_target0</styleUrl>
					<Point>
						<coordinates>53.14999999999999,23.55,0</coordinates>
					</Point>
				</Placemark>
				<Placemark>
					<name>Wafra Oil Field (Partitioned Zone, Onshore)</name>
					<description>Status: Operational
Owner/Operator: ADNOC
Role: The principal shore terminal of the Murban system — onshore crude from the Habshan hub loads here through offshore berths and SPMs, beside the Ruwais refining complex
Berths / vessel size: Multiple deep-water loading points serving VLCC-scale tankers
Storage capacity: Large crude tank farm buffering the onshore pipeline system against liftings
Connectivity: Pipeline-fed from Habshan; the western twin of the ADCOP/Fujairah route — same crude, two exits on opposite sides of the strait
Recent: 2026 — Gulf-side Murban loadings here required escorted transits while Fujairah loadings sailed free: one grade, two terminals, two completely different risk premiums
Strategic role: The inside-the-strait half of the UAE&apos;s dual-exit design — the benchmark of what ADCOP was built to hedge
Risks: Hormuz dependence; coastal concentration beside Ruwais; regional escalation</description>
					<styleUrl>#msn_target0</styleUrl>
					<Point>
						<coordinates>52.58,24.19,0</coordinates>
					</Point>
				</Placemark>
				<Placemark>
					<name>Wafra Oil Field (Partitioned Zone, Onshore)</name>
					<description>Status: Operational
Operator: Kuwait Gulf Oil Company (KGOC) with Saudi Arabian Chevron — production shared 50/50 between Kuwait and Saudi Arabia
Role: The onshore half of the Saudi-Kuwaiti Partitioned Neutral Zone — heavy crude produced under a unique condominium arrangement dating to the 1922 Uqair borders
Capacity: PZ combined design ~500,000 b/d (Wafra plus offshore Khafji); actual output a few hundred thousand, rebuilt since restart
Recent: Shut from May 2015 to early 2020 by a Saudi-Kuwaiti operating dispute — half a million barrels of capacity idled for five years by paperwork, not geology; production resumed after the December 2019 accord and has ramped through the 2020s
Strategic role: The OPEC cut nobody voted for — proof that shared sovereignty is itself an operational risk category
Risks: Bilateral-relations dependence; heavy-crude economics; Chevron concession horizon; Hormuz-dependent evacuation</description>
					<styleUrl>#msn_target0</styleUrl>
					<Point>
						<coordinates>47.93,28.55,0</coordinates>
					</Point>
				</Placemark>
				<Placemark>
					<name>Ahvaz Oil Field</name>
					<description>Status: Reduced
Operator: National Iranian South Oil Company (NISOC)
Role: Iran&apos;s largest oil field — a giant anticline running beneath and around the city of Ahvaz in Khuzestan; producing since the late 1950s; Iranian Heavy and Light export grades
Capacity: ~700,000+ b/d in normal operation — the single biggest contributor to Iranian production
Connectivity: Khuzestan gathering network to Ganaveh and the subsea lines feeding Kharg Island
Recent: 2026 — output throttled toward domestic-refinery demand as the war and US naval blockade shut the export outlet; production reportedly running roughly in line with home consumption
Risks: Export-outlet dependence on Kharg; wartime strike exposure; decades of sanctions-starved investment; urban overlap of field and city</description>
					<styleUrl>#msn_target0</styleUrl>
					<Point>
						<coordinates>48.65,31.3,0</coordinates>
					</Point>
				</Placemark>
				<Placemark>
					<name>Marun Oil Field</name>
					<description>Status: Reduced
Operator: National Iranian South Oil Company (NISOC)
Role: One of Iran&apos;s supergiants, east of Ahvaz in Khuzestan — discovered 1963; a pillar of the Iranian Heavy export stream and feedstock for regional petrochemicals
Capacity: ~520,000 b/d in normal operation
Connectivity: Khuzestan trunklines into the Kharg export system; associated gas to the Marun petrochemical complex
Recent: 2026 — throttled with the rest of the southern system as the blockade closed exports; production aligned to domestic refinery runs
Risks: Kharg single-outlet dependence; wartime exposure; ageing reservoir management under sanctions</description>
					<styleUrl>#msn_target0</styleUrl>
					<Point>
						<coordinates>49.29999999999999,31.1,0</coordinates>
					</Point>
				</Placemark>
				<Placemark>
					<name>Azadegan Oil Field (North &amp; South)</name>
					<description>Status: Reduced
Operator: NISOC / Petroleum Engineering &amp; Development Co.; North Azadegan developed with CNPC, South Azadegan developed domestically after foreign exits
Role: Iran&apos;s largest oil reserves in one structure — the West Karun giant on the Iraqi border, geologically shared with Iraq&apos;s Majnoon; the centrepiece of Iran&apos;s ambition to grow West Karun output
Capacity: ~200,000–250,000 b/d combined in recent years against plateau ambitions of ~570,000 — chronically capital-starved
Connectivity: West Karun gathering toward the Khuzestan trunk system and Kharg
Recent: 2026 — expansion plans frozen by war and sanctions; output constrained to the domestic-demand ceiling like the rest of the south
Risks: Sanctions on investment and technology; border-zone location; the gap between reserves and deliverability — Iran&apos;s structural story in one field</description>
					<styleUrl>#msn_target0</styleUrl>
					<Point>
						<coordinates>47.8,31.05,0</coordinates>
					</Point>
				</Placemark>
				<Placemark id="035F65880D3F3CCC6A37">
					<name>Halfaya Oil Field (CPF-1)</name>
					<description>Status: Operational
Operator: Missan Oil Company; PetroChina-led consortium with TotalEnergies and Petronas
Role: The giant of Missan province — developed since 2010 through three Central Processing Facilities; your point marks CPF-1
Capacity: ~400,000 b/d plateau reached with CPF-3, making Halfaya one of Iraq&apos;s largest post-2003 developments
Connectivity: Missan export pipelines south into the Basra network toward Fao and the Gulf terminals
Recent: 2026 — throttled with the whole southern system by the Hormuz closure and storage saturation
Risks: Hormuz-dependent evacuation; Missan logistics; contractor-model economics under fiscal stress</description>
					<LookAt>
						<longitude>47.4323452</longitude>
						<latitude>31.67494139999999</latitude>
						<altitude>0</altitude>
						<heading>0</heading>
						<tilt>0</tilt>
						<gx:fovy>35</gx:fovy>
						<range>883.1037221287936</range>
						<altitudeMode>relativeToGround</altitudeMode>
					</LookAt>
					<styleUrl>#msn_target0</styleUrl>
					<Point>
						<altitudeMode>relativeToGround</altitudeMode>
						<coordinates>47.4323452,31.6749414,0</coordinates>
					</Point>
				</Placemark>
				<Placemark>
					<name>Rumaila Oil Field</name>
					<description>Status: Reduced
Operator: Basra Oil Company with Basra Energy Company (BP–PetroChina joint venture) as lead contractor
Role: Iraq&apos;s largest oil field and one of the world&apos;s supergiants — North and South Rumaila stretch ~80 km along the Kuwaiti border west of Basra; discovered 1953; the base of the Basrah crude grades
Capacity: ~1.4 million b/d in normal operation — roughly a third of Iraqi production
Connectivity: Degassing stations into the southern pipeline network, Zubair/Fao storage and the Gulf export terminals
Recent: 2026 — output cut back as the Hormuz closure saturated Fao storage within days and forced field shut-ins; national production halved to ~1.9 million b/d by June
Risks: 100% Hormuz-dependent evacuation; storage-saturation shut-ins; ageing water-injection infrastructure; fiscal pressure on the operator model</description>
					<styleUrl>#msn_target0</styleUrl>
					<Point>
						<coordinates>47.34999999999999,30.35,0</coordinates>
					</Point>
				</Placemark>
				<Placemark>
					<name>West Qurna 1 Oil Field</name>
					<description>Status: Reduced
Operator: Basra Oil Company; PetroChina lead contractor since ExxonMobil&apos;s 2024 exit
Role: The northern extension of the Rumaila trend — a supergiant in its own right, ~50 km northwest of Basra
Capacity: ~550,000–600,000 b/d in normal operation, with long-term expansion plans toward much higher plateaus
Connectivity: Southern gathering and pipeline network to Zubair/Fao storage and the Gulf terminals
Recent: 2026 — throttled with the rest of the southern system by the Hormuz closure and storage saturation; PetroChina consolidating its position as the dominant foreign operator in southern Iraq
Risks: Hormuz-dependent single evacuation route; contractor-model economics; infrastructure and water-supply constraints (the Common Seawater Supply Project remains unfinished)</description>
					<styleUrl>#msn_target0</styleUrl>
					<Point>
						<coordinates>47.32931550724308,30.65228549676802,0</coordinates>
					</Point>
				</Placemark>
				<Placemark>
					<name>West Qurna 2 Oil Field</name>
					<description>Status: Reduced
Operator: Basra Oil Company; Lukoil as lead contractor — status in flux
Role: The deeper, western licence of the West Qurna supergiant, developed by Lukoil from 2014
Capacity: ~480,000 b/d in normal operation
Connectivity: Central processing facilities into the southern pipeline and export system
Recent: US sanctions on Lukoil (October 2025) threw the contract into crisis — force majeure declared, payment channels frozen, and Lukoil exploring an exit or sale of its stake while operations continue; the 2026 Hormuz closure then throttled output along with the whole southern system
Risks: Operator uncertainty under sanctions — the largest Russian-operated asset in the Arab world; Hormuz dependence; payment-mechanism fragility; potential operatorship transition</description>
					<styleUrl>#msn_target0</styleUrl>
					<Point>
						<coordinates>47.2,30.85,0</coordinates>
					</Point>
				</Placemark>
				<Placemark>
					<name>Greater Burgan Oil Field</name>
					<description>Status: Operational
Operator: Kuwait Oil Company (KOC)
Role: The world&apos;s second-largest oil field after Ghawar — the Burgan, Magwa and Ahmadi sectors in southeast Kuwait; discovered 1938, producing since 1946; the base of the Kuwait Export Crude blend
Capacity: ~1.6 million b/d sustainable — well over half of Kuwaiti production; medium sour crude gathered through KOC&apos;s numbered gathering centres
Connectivity: Short pipelines north-east to the Ahmadi tank farms and the Mina al-Ahmadi export terminal
Recent: 2026 — with no pipeline bypass, Burgan&apos;s barrels sat fully behind the Hormuz closure; the 1991 wellhead fires remain the field&apos;s defining historical trauma
Risks: Total Hormuz dependence; maturity and water management after eight decades; gathering-centre concentration</description>
					<styleUrl>#msn_target0</styleUrl>
					<Point>
						<coordinates>47.93364218729315,29.02404225611651,0</coordinates>
					</Point>
				</Placemark>
				<Placemark>
					<name>Raudhatain Oil Field</name>
					<description>Status: Operational
Operator: Kuwait Oil Company (KOC)
Role: The anchor of the North Kuwait asset — light crude discovered in 1955, produced together with neighbouring Sabriya through shared gathering and processing
Capacity: Several hundred thousand b/d — with Sabriya, the core of North Kuwait&apos;s ~700,000 b/d production area; lighter grades that upgrade the Kuwait Export Crude blend
Connectivity: North Kuwait gathering centres, then trunklines south to the Ahmadi hub and export system
Recent: Water-injection and facility upgrades sustaining the mature northern fields; heavy-oil development at nearby Ratqa complements the light streams
Risks: Hormuz dependence via the single southern export chain; reservoir maturity; proximity to the Iraqi border zone</description>
					<styleUrl>#msn_target0</styleUrl>
					<Point>
						<coordinates>47.75,29.86,0</coordinates>
					</Point>
				</Placemark>
				<Placemark>
					<name>Sabriya Oil Field</name>
					<description>Status: Operational
Operator: Kuwait Oil Company (KOC)
Role: Raudhatain&apos;s twin in North Kuwait — oil production plus the centre of KOC&apos;s Jurassic non-associated gas development, feeding domestic power as well as the crude blend
Capacity: Oil in the low-to-mid hundreds of thousands b/d within the North Kuwait complex; growing gas and condensate output
Connectivity: Shared North Kuwait gathering and processing with Raudhatain; trunklines south to Ahmadi
Recent: Jurassic gas expansion — Kuwait&apos;s attempt to cut the summer crude burn in its power plants, freeing export barrels
Risks: Hormuz dependence; gas-oil operational interdependence; border-zone location</description>
					<styleUrl>#msn_target0</styleUrl>
					<Point>
						<coordinates>47.89999999999999,29.78,0</coordinates>
					</Point>
				</Placemark>
				<Placemark>
					<name>Bab Oil Field</name>
					<description>Status: Operational
Operator: ADNOC Onshore
Role: Abu Dhabi&apos;s founding field — the emirate&apos;s first commercial oil discovery (drilled from 1954, onstream 1960s), ~150 km southwest of Abu Dhabi city; a pillar of the Murban crude stream
Capacity: ~400,000–450,000 b/d, with sour-gas development alongside
Connectivity: Gathered and processed at the Habshan complex on the field&apos;s northern edge — the entry point of the Murban system and of the ADCOP pipeline to Fujairah
Recent: 2026 — Bab barrels kept flowing to market through the Hormuz closure via the Habshan→Fujairah route, the reason Murban stayed the region&apos;s most reliable export grade
Risks: Maturity management after six decades; sour-gas handling; processing concentration at Habshan</description>
					<styleUrl>#msn_target0</styleUrl>
					<Point>
						<coordinates>53.70070814624188,23.65806611036309,0</coordinates>
					</Point>
				</Placemark>
				<Placemark>
					<name>Ghawar Oil Field</name>
					<description>Status: Operational
Operator: Saudi Aramco
Role: The world&apos;s largest conventional onshore oil field — ~280 km north–south by ~30 km, from Ain Dar and Shedgum through Uthmaniyah and Hawiyah to Haradh; producing since 1951
Capacity: ~3.8 million b/d maximum sustained capacity (2019 bond-prospectus disclosure) — historically more than half of cumulative Saudi production; Arabian Light
Connectivity: Feeds the Abqaiq stabilisation complex, thence Ras Tanura (east) or Petroline to Yanbu (west)
Recent: 2026 — output managed down with exports at ~57% of 2025 rates during the Hormuz closure, with westbound Petroline routing maximised
Risks: Water-cut and pressure management after seven decades; drone/missile exposure of associated GOSPs; single-complex dependence on Abqaiq</description>
					<styleUrl>#msn_target0</styleUrl>
					<Point>
						<coordinates>49.29999999999999,25.2,0</coordinates>
					</Point>
				</Placemark>
				<Placemark>
					<name>Khurais Oil Field</name>
					<description>Status: Operational
Operator: Saudi Aramco
Role: One of the kingdom&apos;s giant onshore fields, ~150 km west of Ghawar toward Riyadh — the 2009 megaproject that added the largest single increment in Aramco history, expanded again in 2019
Capacity: ~1.45 million b/d maximum sustained capacity; Arabian Light
Connectivity: Central processing at Khurais, pipeline ties into the east-coast system and Abqaiq
Recent: Struck alongside Abqaiq in the September 2019 drone/missile attack — production restored within weeks; capacity management through the 2026 export constraints
Risks: Demonstrated vulnerability to precision attack; processing-plant concentration; reservoir management on high sustained rates</description>
					<styleUrl>#msn_target0</styleUrl>
					<Point>
						<coordinates>48.07999999999999,25.1,0</coordinates>
					</Point>
				</Placemark>
				<Placemark>
					<name>Shaybah Oil Field</name>
					<description>Status: Operational
Operator: Saudi Aramco
Role: The Empty Quarter giant — Arabian Extra Light from beneath the Rub&apos; al-Khali dunes on the UAE border, onstream 1998; includes a major NGL recovery plant (2016)
Capacity: ~1.0 million b/d maximum sustained capacity
Connectivity: Pipeline north to the eastern processing and export system — its remoteness means everything (people, power, equipment) flies or drives 800 km of desert
Recent: Attacked by drones in August 2019 (fires at the NGL plant, no production loss) — the strike that previewed Abqaiq; steady operations through the 2026 constraints
Risks: Extreme-environment logistics; long single pipeline exposure; NGL-plant concentration</description>
					<styleUrl>#msn_target0</styleUrl>
					<Point>
						<coordinates>53.96000000000001,22.51,0</coordinates>
					</Point>
				</Placemark>
				<Placemark>
					<name>Bomu Oil Field</name>
					<description>Status: Offline
Operator: Renaissance Africa Energy (ex-SPDC, acquired March 2025); JV — NNPC 55%, Renaissance 30%, TotalEnergies 10%, Eni 5%
Role: Ogoniland field (OML 11), eastern Niger Delta — discovered 1958, shut in since 1993 amid community unrest; historic source field of the Trans Niger Pipeline; 96 wells, 5 flow stations and the Bomu Manifold, one of Africa&apos;s largest gathering points
Capacity: Historic peak ~75,000 b/d before the civil war; effectively zero while shut in
Recent: Renaissance announced in July 2026 the revival of a dormant Nigerian field, and officials have discussed resuming Ogoniland output (&gt;40% of OML 11&apos;s recoverable reserves) — but no report names Bomu specifically as restarted, so the shut-in status stands until confirmed
Risks: Community relations and the Ogoni legacy; pipeline vandalism; restart economics after 30 years idle</description>
					<styleUrl>#msn_target0</styleUrl>
					<Point>
						<coordinates>7.2835,4.6605,0</coordinates>
					</Point>
				</Placemark>
				<Placemark>
					<name>Nembe Field Area (OML 29)</name>
					<description>Status: Operational
Operator: Nembe E&amp;P (ex-Aiteo, which bought OML 29 from Shell in 2015 for $1.7bn), in JV with NNPC
Role: OML 29 fields (Nembe, Santa Barbara, Okoroba, Oloibiri — Nigeria&apos;s first commercial discovery, 1956), Bayelsa State; 983 km², 11 fields; source of the Nembe Creek Trunk Line (100 km, 600,000 b/d nameplate)
Capacity: Reported output diverges — ~150,000 b/d vs ~60,000 b/d in different July 2025 reports — likely real swings from vandalism and theft that have at times cut volumes by up to 90%; ~2.2 billion boe reserve base; a new Central Processing Facility and 17 wells target 200,000 b/d
Risks: Pipeline vandalism and crude theft; single-trunkline evacuation; data opacity</description>
					<styleUrl>#msn_target0</styleUrl>
					<Point>
						<coordinates>6.35,4.53,0</coordinates>
					</Point>
				</Placemark>
				<Placemark>
					<name>Hassi Messaoud Oil Field</name>
					<description>Status: Operational
Operator: Sonatrach (sole operator)
Role: Algeria&apos;s largest producing field, flowing since 1956 — the anchor of the Saharan Blend export grade, gathered at Haoud El Hamra
Capacity: ~350,000 b/d; reserves ~6.4 billion bbl
Recent: Part of Sonatrach&apos;s 2025–30 plan (500 exploration + 950 development wells nationally, incl. a new 60,000 b/d separation and compression centre here); officials warn national reserves (~13 billion bbl) could deplete within ~30 years without new discoveries
Risks: Mature-field decline requiring constant enhanced-recovery investment; Sahel border security; dependence on state investment</description>
