				<Placemark id="06E2D3E90A3F2D157379">
					<name>Kirkuk Oil Field (Baba Gurgur)</name>
					<description>Status: Operational
Operator: North Oil Company; BP under a 2025 redevelopment agreement covering the Kirkuk fields (Baba and Avanah domes, Bai Hassan and adjacent fields)
Role: The historic northern supergiant — Baba Gurgur, drilled in 1927, launched Iraqi oil; the anchor of the northern production system feeding the Iraq–Türkiye export corridor
Capacity: ~250,000–300,000 b/d area production, with BP&apos;s redevelopment targeting recovery of long-underinvested capacity
Connectivity: Injects at Khurmala into the KRG pipeline to Fishkhabur and Ceyhan; the rebuilt federal Kirkuk–Fishkhabur line (Offline, under reconstruction on this map) would restore Baghdad&apos;s own route
Recent: BP operations ramped through 2025–26 — the supermajor&apos;s return to the field it discovered a century ago; the ITP treaty&apos;s July 2026 termination hangs over the export route
Risks: Export-route politics (KRG leverage, Türkiye treaty vacuum); decades of underinvestment; security around Kirkuk</description>
