				<Placemark>
					<name>Lower Zakum Oil Field (Offshore)</name>
					<description>Status: Operational
Operator: ADNOC Offshore — ADNOC 60% with INPEX, TotalEnergies, Eni, PetroChina and an Indian ONGC-led consortium as concession partners
Role: The shallower portion of the giant Zakum structure — one of the world&apos;s largest offshore fields, developed separately from Upper Zakum under its own 2018 concession
Capacity: ~400,000 b/d, with expansion targeted toward ~450,000
Connectivity: Subsea lines to Das Island for processing, storage and export in the Das crude blend
Recent: The 2018 concession round made it a showcase of ADNOC&apos;s partner diversification — Japanese, European, Chinese and Indian capital in one field; 2026 liftings constrained by in-Gulf loading like the rest of the offshore system
Risks: Hormuz dependence via Das Island; multi-partner alignment; shallow-water infrastructure age</description>
