				<Placemark>
					<name>Al-Basra Oil Terminal (ABOT) &amp; SPM Buoys</name>
					<description>Status: Reduced
Owner/Operator: Basra Oil Company / Iraqi Oil Ministry; exports marketed by SOMO
Role: Iraq&apos;s principal crude export terminal — a fixed offshore platform complex plus single-point mooring buoys at the head of the Gulf, loading Basrah Medium and Basrah Heavy for the world
Capacity: ~3.4 million b/d nominal across the terminal and SPM system — normally ~93% of Iraqi exports and ~90% of the state budget
Connectivity: Subsea pipelines from the Fao storage and tank farm; the last link of the chain from Rumaila and West Qurna to water
Recent: 2026 — loadings collapsed to 260,000 b/d in May (8% of the 2025 average) as the Hormuz closure struck; tankers were hit near the loading zone; Iran granted case-by-case passage to some Iraqi tankers in August
Strategic role: The single most consequential loading point of the 2026 crisis — OPEC&apos;s second producer funnelled through one offshore complex behind a closed strait
Risks: 100% Hormuz dependence; naval, mine and drone exposure; no meaningful storage behind it once Fao saturates; single-complex concentration</description>
