				<Placemark>
					<name>Saras Sarroch Refinery (Sardinia)</name>
					<description>Status: Operational
Owner: Saras — controlled by Vitol since 2024, when the trading house bought out the founding Moratti family and delisted the company
Capacity: ~300,000 b/d at Sarroch on the Gulf of Cagliari — one of the Mediterranean&apos;s largest merchant refineries, ~15% of Italian capacity, with a 575 MW gasification power plant integrated
Feedstock: A famously flexible slate bought cargo by cargo on the Med market
Main products: Gasoline, diesel, jet — heavily export-oriented into Mediterranean and Atlantic trade
Markets: The cargo market itself: Saras products are a staple of Med pricing windows
Recent: The Vitol takeover completed the era&apos;s pattern — trading houses buying the merchant refineries that make their cargoes (Trafigura at ISAB&apos;s side, Vitol here and at ATAŞ)
Strategic role: The purest merchant refinery in the pricing zone — no captive market, no captive crude: the plant that lives entirely on the spreads this hub&apos;s assessments describe
Risks: Total margin exposure; single-site company; trader-ownership concentration of Med refining</description>
					<styleUrl>#msn_volcano</styleUrl>
					<Point>
						<coordinates>9.017962924003253,39.0755076496107,0</coordinates>
					</Point>
				</Placemark>
				<Placemark>
					<name>BP Whiting Refinery</name>
					<description>Status: Operational
Owner: BP
Capacity: ~435,000 b/d — the largest refinery in the US Midwest, on Lake Michigan at the edge of the Chicago conurbation
Feedstock: Canadian heavy above all — the 2013 modernisation rebuilt it around oil-sands crude delivered by the Enbridge Mainline system
Main products: Gasoline, diesel and jet for the Chicago and Great Lakes market
Markets: The Midwest demand heartland
Recent: A February 2024 power outage shut the whole plant for weeks and moved Midwest gasoline prices — the demonstration of single-plant regional dependence
Strategic role: The largest single customer of the Hardisty barrel — the demand anchor that justifies the whole Mainline corridor on this map
Risks: Regional over-dependence on one plant; heavy-crude supply politics; Lake Michigan environmental scrutiny</description>
