				<Placemark>
					<name>Phillips 66 / Cenovus Wood River Refinery</name>
					<description>Status: Operational
Owner: WRB Refining — Phillips 66 operator, 50/50 with Cenovus
Capacity: ~356,000 b/d on the Mississippi opposite St. Louis — the anchor of the Wood River terminal junction
Feedstock: Canadian heavy (the Cenovus stake exists precisely to place oil-sands barrels) plus Cushing-system light via Ozark
Main products: Gasoline, diesel, jet, asphalt for the Midwest
Markets: St. Louis and the central Midwest
Strategic role: The refinery whose appetite made Wood River a hub — a JV that vertically integrates a Canadian producer into US refining, the demand-side answer to the Hardisty story
Risks: Heavy-light differential swings; Mississippi flood exposure; regional margin cycles</description>
					<styleUrl>#msn_volcano0</styleUrl>
					<Point>
						<coordinates>-90.07014680501794,38.84145802070584,0</coordinates>
					</Point>
				</Placemark>
				<Placemark>
					<name>ExxonMobil Baton Rouge Refinery</name>
					<description>Status: Operational
Owner: ExxonMobil
Capacity: ~520,000 b/cd — among the ten largest US refineries, operating since 1909 on the Mississippi&apos;s east bank
Feedstock: Domestic and imported grades by river dock, pipeline and the St. James hub downstream
Main products: Fuels plus one of the world&apos;s great chemical integrations — the refinery feeds adjacent olefins, aromatics, polymer and resin-finishing plants and a lubricants business across one urban complex
Markets: US Gulf and Midwest systems; chemical exports
Recent: Capacity trimmed from ~540,000 in the 2020s as the site optimised toward chemicals — the American mirror of the fuels-to-chemicals shift your Asian refineries show
Strategic role: The upriver anchor of the Louisiana refining row — a century-old complex that turned into a chemistry campus
Risks: Mississippi flood and hurricane exposure; urban interface; ageing-unit management at scale</description>
					<styleUrl>#msn_volcano3</styleUrl>
					<Point>
						<coordinates>-91.18000000000002,30.48,0</coordinates>
					</Point>
				</Placemark>
				<Placemark>
					<name>Valero St. Charles Refinery</name>
					<description>Status: Operational
Owner: Valero Energy
Capacity: ~340,000 b/d throughput at Norco on the river&apos;s west bank — the largest plant in Valero&apos;s Gulf system
Feedstock: Heavy sour and medium grades — deep conversion with coking and hydrocracking
Main products: Gasoline, diesel, jet — heavily export-oriented toward Latin America
Markets: US Gulf and the Atlantic Basin product trade
Strategic role: The export specialist of the river row — Louisiana products loading straight into the hemispheric market
Risks: Hurricane corridor (Ida 2021 shut the river row for weeks); heavy-crude supply economics; export-demand cycles</description>
					<styleUrl>#msn_volcano3</styleUrl>
					<Point>
						<coordinates>-90.44,29.98,0</coordinates>
					</Point>
				</Placemark>
				<Placemark>
					<name>ExxonMobil Baytown Complex</name>
					<description>Status: Operational
Owner: ExxonMobil
Capacity: ~560,000–588,000 b/d across a ~3,400-acre complex on the Houston Ship Channel — one of the largest integrated refining-petrochemical sites on Earth, operating since 1919
Feedstock: Domestic and imported crudes via the Ship Channel and Texas pipeline grid
Main products: Fuels plus a giant chemical side — olefins, aromatics, polymers, with over 22,000 workers and contractors on site
Markets: US domestic and exports through the Houston complex
Recent: A 2025 explosion at the olefins plant underscored the risk side of extreme integration; a large low-carbon hydrogen project has been planned for the site
Strategic role: ExxonMobil&apos;s home-base flagship — refinery, chemicals and research on one fence line inside the Houston pricing ring
Risks: Industrial incidents at integrated scale; hurricane and channel exposure; petrochemical margin weakness</description>
					<styleUrl>#msn_volcano3</styleUrl>
					<Point>
						<coordinates>-95.01000000000001,29.74,0</coordinates>
					</Point>
				</Placemark>
				<Placemark>
					<name>Mina Abdullah Refinery</name>
					<description>Status: Operational
Owner: Kuwait National Petroleum Company (KNPC)
Capacity: 454,000 b/d — the larger half of the merged 800,000 b/d KNPC complex created with Mina al-Ahmadi under the ~$16bn Clean Fuels Project
Feedstock: Kuwaiti crude from the Ahmadi gathering system
Main products: Euro-5 gasoline and ultra-low-sulphur diesel, jet fuel, LPG, low-sulphur fuel oil, petcoke, sulphur
Markets: Domestic supply and Asian/European product exports via the Shuaiba oil berth and the refinery&apos;s Sea Island
Recent: Running as the volume engine of the merged complex while KNPC pushes combined capacity from ~798,600 toward 825,000 b/d; the 2026 war put the whole coastal strip inside the drone-threat perimeter demonstrated at Mina al-Ahmadi in March
Strategic role: With MAA and Al Zour, one of the three pillars that make Kuwait a top-tier product exporter despite having no crude bypass
Risks: Hormuz dependence for every cargo; single coastal strip concentration; drone and missile exposure</description>
