				<Placemark>
					<name>ENEOS Mizushima Refinery</name>
					<description>Status: Operational
Owner: ENEOS
Capacity: 350,200 b/d — Japan&apos;s largest refinery, formed by integrating two adjacent plants (A and B, both dating to 1961) after the JX-era mergers; deep conversion on both trains (hydrocracking on A, coking on B)
Feedstock: Imported crude via the Port of Mizushima on the Seto Inland Sea
Main products: Full fuel slate, lubricant base oils (ENEOS&apos;s only remaining base-oil site — 411,000 t/yr — after Negishi closed 2022 and Wakayama 2023), petroleum coke, naphtha and aromatics
Markets: Nationwide shipping; feeds the surrounding Mizushima petrochemical, steel and power complex directly
Recent: 2025 — the B-train&apos;s No. 3 CDU in scheduled maintenance from February, its No. 2 CDU hit by an unplanned April shutdown; ENEOS had studied closing Kashima or Mizushima in 2020 — both still run
Strategic role: The volume anchor of Japanese refining and of the Seto Inland Sea industrial belt
Risks: Two-train complexity and outage exposure; national demand decline; earthquake/tsunami exposure on the Inland Sea</description>
