				<Placemark id="ADDPCK00000000000001">
					<name>PCK Schwedt Refinery</name>
					<description>Status: Reduced
Owner: Rosneft ~54% (under German federal trusteeship since Sept 2022; permanent state control cleared by the European Commission in 2026), Shell ~37.5%, Eni ~8%
Capacity: ~11.6 Mt/yr (~230,000 b/d nameplate); running below capacity since the loss of Russian pipeline crude
Feedstock: Historically Russian Urals via Druzhba (ended Jan 2023); now seaborne crude via the Rostock–Schwedt pipeline and Gdańsk; Kazakh transit via Druzhba halted by Russia from May 2026
Main products: Gasoline, diesel, jet fuel, heating oil, bitumen
Markets: ~90% of fuels for Berlin and Brandenburg, incl. Berlin airport (BER)
Recent: May 2026 — Russia halted Kazakh transit; Germany secured a US sanctions exemption; ~€400m Rostock-route upgrade pursued
Strategic role: The centrepiece of Germany&apos;s post-2022 crude rerouting — where the Druzhba cut-off became a live logistics problem
Risks: Dependence on the small Rostock pipeline; ownership limbo; feedstock diversification costs; utilisation and margins</description>
