				<Placemark>
					<name>Mina al-Ahmadi refinery</name>
					<description>Status: Operational
Owner: Kuwait National Petroleum Company (KNPC, Kuwait Petroleum Corporation)
Capacity: 346,000 b/d — Kuwait&apos;s first refinery (1949, started at 25,000 b/d), 10.5 km² along the Gulf shore; integrated with Mina Abdullah (454,000 b/d) into one 800,000 b/d merchant complex under the ~$16bn Clean Fuels Project
Feedstock: Kuwaiti crude piped from the adjacent Ahmadi gathering and tank-farm system
Main products: Euro-5 gasoline and ultra-low-sulphur diesel, jet fuel, LPG (five liquefaction trains), naphtha, low-sulphur marine fuel, petcoke, sulphur; the on-site gas plant processes 3.1 bcf/d plus ~332,000 b/d of condensate
Markets: Kuwait&apos;s domestic demand and Asian/European product exports via the refinery&apos;s own piers
Recent: Repeated Iranian drone attacks during the 2026 war — a March 2026 strike set fires in multiple units, since repaired; KNPC is pushing the two-refinery complex from ~798,600 b/d toward 825,000 with unit-by-unit debottlenecking
Strategic role: The refining heart of the Ahmadi complex — crude blending, gas processing, products and export piers on one site
Risks: Drone and missile exposure demonstrated; single-complex concentration with the export terminal; Hormuz dependence for every outbound cargo</description>
