			<Folder>
				<name>Refineries</name>
				<Placemark>
					<name>Lukoil Neftochim Burgas Refinery (Bulgaria)</name>
					<description>Status: Operational
Owner: Lukoil — but under Bulgarian state control since late 2025: a special commercial manager was appointed under emergency legislation, with a US sanctions waiver keeping operations and a sale process alive
Capacity: ~190,000 b/d — the largest refinery in the Black Sea region and Bulgaria&apos;s dominant industrial asset, supplying most of the national fuel market
Feedstock: Non-Russian seaborne crude since the EU derogation ended; landed at the Rosenets terminal on Burgas bay
Main products: The Bulgarian fuel slate plus regional exports
Recent: The mirror of Romania&apos;s Petrotel — same sanctioned owner, opposite outcome: Sofia seized operational control and kept the plant running while Bucharest&apos;s lighter supervision watched Petrotel freeze into insolvency
Strategic role: Two Black Sea refineries, one Russian owner, two legal experiments — the pair teaches how much sovereign choices, not sanctions alone, decide an asset&apos;s fate
Risks: Ownership limbo and sale execution; waiver renewals; single-refinery national dependence</description>
					<styleUrl>#msn_R</styleUrl>
					<Point>
						<coordinates>27.33518851353852,42.5455278622034,0</coordinates>
					</Point>
				</Placemark>
				<Placemark>
					<name>Petromidia Refinery (Rompetrol)</name>
					<description>Status: Operational
Owner: Rompetrol Rafinare — KMG International, the Kazakh state oil company&apos;s European arm
Capacity: ~100,000 b/d (5.9 Mt/yr in 2025, 5.4 Mt of crude run) — Romania&apos;s largest refinery, on the Black Sea at Năvodari beside its own petrochemical plant
Feedstock: Seaborne crude, Kazakh KEBCO prominent — KMG feeding its own refinery, landed at the Midia offshore buoy
Main products: ~27% of Romania&apos;s gasoline and diesel consumption (1.66 Mt gasoline, 2.52 Mt diesel in 2025), plus jet, LPG and polymers
Markets: Romania and Black Sea regional trade
Recent: A 20-day March 2026 turnaround became a political incident — the energy minister claimed to have postponed it while the sanctions crisis squeezed national supply; back at full capacity since April
Strategic role: Kazakhstan&apos;s downstream anchor in the EU — and the swing supplier when a fifth of Romanian refining went dark
Risks: Black Sea war environment (drones and mines offshore); single-buoy crude supply; carrying national supply when others fail</description>
					<styleUrl>#default2</styleUrl>
					<Point>
						<coordinates>28.6555,44.3404,0</coordinates>
					</Point>
				</Placemark>
				<Placemark>
					<name>Petrobrazi Refinery (OMV Petrom)</name>
					<description>Status: Operational
Owner: OMV Petrom
Capacity: ~90,000 b/d (4.5 Mt/yr) at Brazi, south of Ploiești — sized to process the company&apos;s entire Romanian equity crude
Feedstock: Domestic crude from OMV Petrom&apos;s onshore fields, topped with imports — the only Romanian refinery still living mainly off Romanian oil
Main products: About a third of national fuel demand; the Polyfuel unit converts LPG and low-grade gasoline into high-octane product
Markets: Romania via OMV Petrom&apos;s retail network
Recent: Run at maximum capacity through the 2026 supply squeeze after Petrotel went dark
Strategic role: The integrated national champion — field-to-forecourt within one company and one country, the rarest configuration in modern Europe
Risks: Domestic-crude decline; single-site dependence for OMV Petrom&apos;s refining</description>
					<styleUrl>#default2</styleUrl>
					<Point>
						<coordinates>26.01144429804088,44.87272804571819,0</coordinates>
					</Point>
				</Placemark>
				<Placemark>
					<name>Petrotel-Lukoil Refinery</name>
					<description>Status: Offline
Owner: Lukoil (since 1998) — under extended Romanian state supervision since 12 February 2026; assets up for sale under sanctions pressure
Capacity: ~48,000 b/d (2.7 Mt/yr) at Ploiești — founded 1904, the oldest of Lukoil&apos;s European refineries; historically ~21% of Romanian fuel production
Feedstock: Non-Russian crude only since December 2022, delivered by rail and by pipeline from Constanța
Recent: Stopped in October 2025 for a scheduled overhaul that US sanctions never allowed to restart; the government sought an informal OFAC permit to reopen it in March 2026 without success; entered insolvency on 24 August 2026 to protect the plant, its workers and creditors
Strategic role: The map&apos;s clearest case of sanctions reaching inside the EU — a Russian-owned refinery frozen solid on NATO territory, its fate decided in Washington, its workers&apos; in Ploiești
Risks: Sale or dismantlement; restart complexity after a year cold; the precedent it sets for every Lukoil asset in Europe</description>
					<styleUrl>#default2</styleUrl>
					<Point>
						<coordinates>26.0814,44.9464,0</coordinates>
					</Point>
				</Placemark>
				<Placemark>
					<name>Vega Refinery Ploiești (Rompetrol)</name>
					<description>Status: Operational
Owner: Rompetrol Rafinare (KMG International)
Capacity: ~350,000 t/yr — small, but Romania&apos;s oldest operating refinery (1905), and a specialist rather than a fuels plant
Feedstock: Intermediate streams railed from Petromidia — the two Rompetrol plants operate as one system
Main products: Bitumen, solvents, hexane, special fuels — niche products the big plants don&apos;t make
Recent: Shares Petromidia&apos;s maintenance calendar (the March 2026 joint turnaround)
Strategic role: The survivor of Ploiești&apos;s century — the town that hosted the world&apos;s first industrial refining (1857) and the WWII Tidal Wave raids keeps one working heir
Risks: Niche-scale economics; dependence on Petromidia feedstock</description>
					<styleUrl>#default2</styleUrl>
					<Point>
						<coordinates>26.03,44.96,0</coordinates>
					</Point>
				</Placemark>
				<Placemark>
					<name>Sonatrach Augusta Refinery</name>
					<description>Status: Operational
Owner: Sonatrach — Algeria&apos;s state oil company; its only refinery in the EU, bought from ExxonMobil in 2018
Capacity: ~190,000 b/d on Augusta Bay, refining since 1950
Feedstock: Algerian Saharan Blend prominently — the captive outlet strategy: Sonatrach placing its own crude in its own European plant
Main products: Gasoline, diesel, jet, LPG, lubricant base oils (a historic strength of the site)
Markets: Italy and Mediterranean cargoes
Strategic role: The second pillar of Augusta Bay — and the Mediterranean mirror of Motiva Port Arthur on this map: a producer country buying its customer; ties directly to the Skikda and Haoud El Hamra assets upstream
Risks: Single-owner strategic alignment; ageing units; Mediterranean margin competition</description>
					<styleUrl>#default2</styleUrl>
					<Point>
						<coordinates>15.18487431037399,37.17706742974956,0</coordinates>
					</Point>
				</Placemark>
				<Placemark>
					<name>RAM Milazzo Refinery</name>
					<description>Status: Operational
Owner: Raffineria di Milazzo — Eni 50%, Kuwait Petroleum Italia 50%
Capacity: ~241,000 b/d on Sicily&apos;s north coast — the island&apos;s third refinery, facing the Tyrrhenian rather than the Augusta cluster
Feedstock: Middle Eastern (Kuwaiti barrels via the KPC stake) and Mediterranean grades
Main products: Gasoline, diesel, jet, fuel oil for Italy and Med export
Markets: Italian mainland supply and cargo trade
Strategic role: Kuwait&apos;s European refining foothold — the same customer-equity model as OQ8 Duqm on this map, executed in Sicily; completes the island&apos;s three-refinery system
Risks: JV alignment; merchant margins; the coast&apos;s seismic and volcanic neighbourhood</description>
					<styleUrl>#default2</styleUrl>
					<Point>
						<coordinates>15.2745367499415,38.20101066725885,0</coordinates>
					</Point>
				</Placemark>
				<Placemark>
					<name>Shell Norco Refinery</name>
					<description>Status: Operational
Owner: Shell
Capacity: ~250,000 b/d on the river&apos;s east bank at Norco — the name itself is oil history: New Orleans Refining Company, 1916
Feedstock: Domestic, offshore Gulf and imported grades by river dock and pipeline
Main products: Gasoline, diesel, jet; deeply integrated with the adjacent Shell chemicals complex
Markets: Gulf system and exports via the river
Recent: Shell sold its other Louisiana plants (Convent closed 2020) and concentrated its US Gulf refining here — the keeper of the portfolio
Strategic role: The fourth pillar of the river refining row, completing the Baton Rouge–Garyville–St. Charles–Norco line on this map
Risks: Hurricane corridor (Ida 2021 blacked out the row); urban-industrial interface; single-site concentration for Shell&apos;s US refining</description>
					<styleUrl>#default2</styleUrl>
					<Point>
						<coordinates>-90.397902772759,29.99639669733044,0</coordinates>
					</Point>
				</Placemark>
				<Placemark>
					<name>Marathon Robinson Refinery</name>
					<description>Status: Operational
Owner: Marathon Petroleum
Capacity: ~253,000 b/d in eastern Illinois, pipeline-fed from the Patoka system
Feedstock: Domestic and Canadian grades drawn from the hub next door
Main products: Gasoline, distillates, asphalt for the Illinois-Indiana market
Markets: Central Midwest
Strategic role: Patoka&apos;s local customer — the refinery whose demand competes with Capline and ETCOP for the hub&apos;s barrels; when Robinson runs full, fewer northern barrels head south
Risks: Mid-continent margin cycles; single-region product placement</description>
					<styleUrl>#default2</styleUrl>
					<Point>
						<coordinates>-87.72419691897159,38.99926764571771,0</coordinates>
					</Point>
				</Placemark>
				<Placemark>
					<name>Phillips 66 Ponca City Refinery</name>
					<description>Status: Operational
Owner: Phillips 66
Capacity: ~200,000 b/d — the historic Conoco flagship, refining continuously since 1918, ~90 km north of Cushing
Feedstock: Mid-Continent and Permian crudes drawn from the Cushing hub system
Main products: Gasoline, diesel, jet fuel, LPG, specialty products for the Mid-Continent market
Markets: Oklahoma, Kansas and the surrounding region by product pipeline and rack
Strategic role: The demand anchor closest to the benchmark — a reminder that Cushing began as a refining town before it became a tank farm; local refining runs are one of the draws on hub inventories
Risks: Mid-Continent margin cycles; single-plant regional concentration; competition from Gulf Coast mega-refineries</description>
