					<name>Baiji refinery</name>
					<description>Status: Operational
Owner: North Refineries Company (Iraqi Ministry of Oil)
Capacity: ~290,000–310,000 b/d nominal for the full Al-Sumood complex; a rehabilitated 150,000 b/d North Refinery restarted Feb 2024 after ~10 years offline, bringing restored capacity to ~290,000 b/d
Feedstock: Iraqi crude via the national pipeline network (northern and central fields)
Main products: Gasoline, diesel, kerosene, jet, LPG, naphtha, fuel oil, sulphur
Markets: Baghdad and northern/central Iraq
Recent: Rehabilitation continuing; early-2026 reporting credited Baiji with much of Iraq&apos;s rising refining capacity. A Feb 2026 fire at a fuel unit was contained (one fatality)
Strategic role: Historically Iraq&apos;s largest refining centre — key to cutting gasoline and diesel imports
Risks: War-damaged, ageing infrastructure; fires; power failures; pipeline interruptions; security; nominal vs actual throughput</description>
