				<Placemark>
					<name>Pancevo refinery</name>
					<description>Status: Operational
Owner: NIS — Russian-controlled since 2008 (Gazprom Neft 44.9%, Gazprom 11.3%, Serbian state 29.9%); MOL agreed in Jan 2026 to buy the 56.15% Russian stake (~€1bn), ADNOC as possible minority partner — pending OFAC approval (final stage, Aug 2026)
Capacity: ~4.8 Mt/yr (~96,000 b/d); hydrocracker (2012) and delayed coker (2020); supplies ~80% of Serbia&apos;s diesel and gasoline
Feedstock: Imported crude via Croatia&apos;s JANAF pipeline from Omišalj — the only supply route; ~20% from NIS&apos;s Serbian fields; Russian crude excluded since 2022
Main products: Euro-5 diesel and gasoline, jet, LPG, bitumen
Markets: Serbia
Recent: US sanctions on NIS took effect 9 Oct 2025 — JANAF halted deliveries, hot standby from 25 Nov; restarted Jan 2026 under OFAC waivers; MOL committed to run it ≥10 years at pre-sanctions capacity
Strategic role: Serbia&apos;s only refinery — a landlocked country&apos;s single fuel source, and one of Russia&apos;s last European energy assets, now passing to MOL (to be linked with Bratislava and Százhalombatta)
Risks: Single supply route (JANAF); sanctions and ownership uncertainty until closing; Danube low water limits barge alternatives</description>
