				<Placemark>
					<name>Pakistan Refinery Limited, Korangi Creek</name>
					<description>Status: Operational
Owner: Pakistan State Oil (63.56%) — state-controlled via the national oil marketer
Capacity: ~50,000 b/d (2.13 Mt/yr nominal); operating since 1962, one of Pakistan&apos;s oldest refineries
Feedstock: Mostly imported crude via the company&apos;s Keamari terminal on Karachi harbour, plus small domestic volumes; first Nigerian Bonny Light cargo bought from Vitol in 2025 — a diversification away from the Gulf
Main products: High-speed diesel, gasoline (isomerisation unit), jet fuel, kerosene, naphtha, LPG, furnace oil; a dedicated pipeline supplies Karachi Airport
Markets: Karachi and southern Pakistan
Recent: The Refinery Expansion &amp; Upgrade Project (REUP) — $1.8–2bn, the sector&apos;s most ambitious — would double capacity to 100,000 b/d and eliminate furnace-oil output; FEED complete, EPC engagement under way
Strategic role: The state&apos;s own refinery — PSO integration makes it the policy instrument of Pakistani fuel security
Risks: Negative refining margins in weak years; financing a project 20× its equity; execution risk; Karachi port dependence</description>
