				<Placemark>
					<name>Santa Panagia Marine Terminal</name>
					<description>Status: Operational
Owner/Operator: ISAB system
Role: The deep-water terminal of the Priolo complex — jetties and offshore moorings in Santa Panagia bay handling the largest tankers serving the refinery, south of the main Augusta harbour
Berths / vessel size: Deep-water berths and moorings up to VLCC-scale crude deliveries
Connectivity: Pipeline-linked into the three-site ISAB complex and its tank farms
Strategic role: The heavy-lift entrance of Italy&apos;s largest refinery — the point where a fifth of the country&apos;s crude intake touches land
Risks: Single-terminal dependence for ISAB&apos;s largest deliveries; exposed-bay weather; spill sensitivity on a tourist coast</description>
					<styleUrl>#msn_red-circle</styleUrl>
					<Point>
						<coordinates>15.25793616711022,37.10856487298696,0</coordinates>
					</Point>
				</Placemark>
				<Placemark>
					<name>Hardisty Terminal &amp; Blending Hub</name>
					<description>Status: Operational
Owner/Operator: Multiple — TC Energy, Enbridge and Gibson Energy tank farms side by side in a small Alberta town
Role: Canada&apos;s Cushing — the tank-farm and blending hub where oil-sands bitumen is mixed with condensate diluent into Western Canadian Select (WCS) and other export blends
Storage capacity: Tens of millions of barrels across the operators&apos; farms
Connectivity: The origin of the Keystone Mainline and a major feed of the Enbridge Mainline — the departure lounge of Canadian heavy toward the US Midwest and Gulf
Recent: The WCS–WTI differential assessed at Hardisty is Canada&apos;s most-watched price — it widens whenever pipelines fill and narrows since TMX opened a Pacific exit in 2024
Strategic role: The pricing point of a landlocked barrel — the mirror image of Cushing at the other end of the Keystone system, and the fifth national gathering hinge on this map after Abqaiq, Habshan, Haoud El Hamra and Saih Nihayda
Risks: Pipeline-capacity dependence (the discount is the symptom); diluent supply economics; concentration of Canada&apos;s export system in one town</description>
					<styleUrl>#msn_red-circle5</styleUrl>
					<Point>
						<coordinates>-111.270083510404,52.64156129550192,0</coordinates>
					</Point>
				</Placemark>
				<Placemark>
					<name>Ras Markaz Crude Oil Park</name>
					<description>Status: Operational
Owner/Operator: Oman Tank Terminal Company (OTTCO, OQ group)
Role: The Gulf region&apos;s outside-Hormuz commercial crude storage play — a tank-farm park on the open Arabian Sea coast south of Duqm, master-planned toward world-scale capacity in phases
Storage capacity: First phase in service (supplying the Duqm refinery among others), with a long-term master plan measured in tens of millions of barrels
Connectivity: Marine import/export via SPM; pipeline link toward the Duqm refinery and port zone
Recent: 2026 made its pitch for it — storage that no blockade, strait or convoy schedule can touch; exactly the product the region&apos;s traders suddenly wanted
Strategic role: The physical expression of the lesson every 2026 fiche on this map teaches — location relative to chokepoints is the asset
Risks: Phased build-out economics; remote-site costs; competition from established hubs (Fujairah) for third-party customers</description>
					<styleUrl>#msn_red-circle50</styleUrl>
					<Point>
						<coordinates>57.7445697750924,19.17015049337974,0</coordinates>
					</Point>
				</Placemark>
				<Placemark>
					<name>Das Island Terminal</name>
					<description>Status: Operational
Owner/Operator: ADNOC Offshore and ADNOC LNG
Role: The historic offshore hub of Abu Dhabi — processing, storage and export island for the Umm Shaif and Lower Zakum fields (the Das crude blend), plus the Gulf&apos;s first LNG plant, exporting since 1977
Berths / vessel size: Deep-water crude berths and LNG jetties inside the Gulf
Connectivity: Subsea pipelines from the surrounding fields; sister island Zirku handles the Upper Zakum system nearby
Recent: 2026 — like Zirku, an in-Gulf loading point on the wrong side of the strait: liftings moved under escorted transits and STS workarounds while pipeline-served Murban flowed freely from Fujairah
Strategic role: Fifty years of offshore logistics on one island — and half of the demonstration (with Zirku) of why ADNOC built ADCOP for the onshore barrels
Risks: Hormuz dependence; island concentration of crude and LNG; ageing first-generation infrastructure</description>
					<styleUrl>#msn_red-circle4</styleUrl>
					<Point>
						<coordinates>52.87,25.15,0</coordinates>
					</Point>
				</Placemark>
				<Placemark>
					<name>Kharg Island Export Terminal</name>
					<description>Status: Reduced
Owner/Operator: National Iranian Oil Company / IRGC-controlled island
Role: The gate of Iranian oil — ~90% of crude exports load at this small coral island&apos;s deep-water jetties (T-jetty east, Sea Island west, capacity to load ten supertankers); the deep berths no other Iranian port can replicate
Storage capacity: Large tank farms buffering the Khuzestan pipeline inflow — filled to near-zero spare during the blockade
Recent: The defining asset of the 2026 war — 13 March: US raid on 90+ military sites, oil plant deliberately spared; 13 April–29 May: naval blockade, first prolonged loading halt, storage saturating; June window: 40–70 million barrels shipped in three weeks; blockade reimposed mid-July, berths empty from 31 July; limited western-jetty restart 12 August; repeated oil slicks offshore
Strategic role: Leverage made physical — spared as a bargaining chip, blockaded as a tourniquet; the purest demonstration on this map that access, not infrastructure, is what gets switched off
Risks: Strike or seizure escalation; blockade reimposition; environmental incidents from distressed tanker operations; total national dependence on one island</description>
					<styleUrl>#msn_red-circle0</styleUrl>
					<Point>
						<coordinates>50.31,29.245,0</coordinates>
					</Point>
				</Placemark>
				<Placemark>
					<name>Mina al-Ahmadi Export Complex &amp; Ahmadi Tank Farm</name>
					<description>Status: Operational
Owner/Operator: Kuwait Petroleum Corporation system (KOC tank farms, KNPC refinery, KPC marketing)
Role: The single gate of Kuwaiti oil — the Ahmadi ridge tank farms blend Burgan and northern crudes into Kuwait Export Crude, feeding the Mina al-Ahmadi piers and offshore sea island; the adjacent Mina al-Ahmadi refinery serves domestic and export products
Berths / vessel size: Piers and deep-water sea island loading up to VLCC scale
Connectivity: Gathering centres from all Kuwaiti fields converge here; Al Zour to the south handles the newest refining
Recent: 2026 — Kuwait&apos;s exports throttled by the Hormuz closure with no pipeline alternative; the complex is the country&apos;s entire export capacity in one place
Strategic role: Kuwait&apos;s Abqaiq and Ras Tanura combined — blending hub, tank farm and export terminal on one ridge and shoreline
Risks: Absolute single-point concentration; Hormuz dependence; industrial and drone exposure; the 1991 precedent of deliberate infrastructure destruction</description>
					<styleUrl>#msn_red-circle0</styleUrl>
					<Point>
						<coordinates>48.14,29.06,0</coordinates>
					</Point>
				</Placemark>
				<Placemark>
					<name>Zirku Island Terminal</name>
					<description>Status: Operational
Owner/Operator: ADNOC Offshore
Role: The processing, storage and export island of the Upper Zakum system — crude from the field&apos;s artificial islands is stabilised, stored and loaded onto tankers here; the loading point of the Upper Zakum grade
Berths / vessel size: Deep-water berths serving VLCC-scale liftings inside the Gulf
Connectivity: Subsea pipelines from Upper Zakum; sister island Das serves the Lower Zakum/Umm Shaif system nearby
Recent: 2026 — as an in-Gulf terminal it sat on the wrong side of the strait: liftings depended on escorted transits and STS workarounds off Oman, unlike Fujairah-loaded Murban
Strategic role: The offshore half of the UAE export system — and the demonstration of why ADNOC is planning a second pipeline outside Hormuz
Risks: Hormuz dependence; island-terminal concentration; regional naval and drone incidents</description>
					<styleUrl>#msn_red-circle0</styleUrl>
					<Point>
						<coordinates>53.07,24.88,0</coordinates>
					</Point>
				</Placemark>
				<Placemark id="ADDEILT000000000001">
					<name>EAPC Eilat Oil Terminal</name>
					<description>Status: Operational
Owner/Operator: Europe Asia Pipeline Company (Israeli state-owned)
Role: Red Sea gate of the Trans-Israel pipeline — lets Gulf-origin crude reach the Mediterranean without transiting Suez, and reverse flows toward Asia
Berths / vessel size: Tankers up to ~350,000 dwt (VLCC-capable); oil jetty within the Eilat port area
Storage capacity: Up to ~1.4 million m³
Recent: Constrained by the Environment Ministry&apos;s &quot;Zero Additional Risk&quot; policy (Nov 2021) protecting the Gulf&apos;s coral reefs — froze the UAE Red-Med ramp-up; the Red Sea shipping crisis since late 2023 further curtailed calls
Risks: A single spill next to a fragile coral ecosystem — EAPC caused the 2014 Arava spill and a 2016 coral-damage case; Bab el-Mandeb security; regulatory cap on growth</description>
					<styleUrl>#msn_red-circle0</styleUrl>
					<Point>
						<coordinates>34.93477390244553,29.52766276554422,0</coordinates>
					</Point>
				</Placemark>
				<Placemark id="ADDASHK000000000001">
					<name>EAPC Ashkelon Oil Terminal &amp; Tank Farm</name>
					<description>Status: Operational
Owner/Operator: Europe Asia Pipeline Company
Role: Mediterranean gate of the Trans-Israel pipeline and Israel&apos;s main crude import point — the hinge between seaborne supply and the Ashdod and Haifa refineries
Berths / vessel size: Offshore moorings; tankers up to ~250,000 dwt
Storage capacity: ~2.3 million m³ of crude plus ~410,000 m³ of products across two product terminals
Crude flows: Azeri BTC and Kazakh CPC grades prominent among imports; potential reverse flows toward Eilat
Risks: ~10 km from Gaza — loadings interrupted in May 2021, precautionary measures after October 2023; war-risk insurance costs; single-point concentration of Israeli crude imports</description>
					<styleUrl>#msn_red-circle0</styleUrl>
					<Point>
						<coordinates>34.543,31.642,0</coordinates>
					</Point>
				</Placemark>
				<Placemark id="ADDGOR00000000000001">
					<name>Rostock Oil Harbour &amp; GÖR Tank Farm (Euroports)</name>
					<description>Status: Operational
Owner/Operator: Großtanklager-Ölhafen Rostock (GÖR), Euroports Germany subsidiary; GÖR also operates PCK Schwedt&apos;s and Dow Böhlen&apos;s on-site tanks; PCK owns its own crude tank farm and pier
Role: Baltic crude and products gateway — head of the Rostock–Schwedt crude line (MVL) and the Böhlen product line
Berths / vessel size: Six berths for vessels up to ~100,000 t (Aframax scale); discharge up to 6,000 t/hour
Storage capacity: ~700,000 m³; rail loading (7 tracks), automated truck loading; ~200,000 t/yr biodiesel plant on site
Recent: ~6 Mt/yr of crude pumped into the Schwedt pipeline since Germany ended Russian pipeline imports — securing eastern Germany&apos;s and Berlin&apos;s fuel supply
Risks: Berth cap at Aframax scale; throughput bottleneck versus Schwedt&apos;s nameplate; Baltic weather; single-corridor dependence</description>
					<styleUrl>#msn_red-circle0</styleUrl>
					<Point>
						<coordinates>12.14519945455248,54.14954764676539,0</coordinates>
					</Point>
				</Placemark>
				<Placemark id="ADDDAL00000000000001">
					<name>Dalmeny Tank Farm (FPS)</name>
					<description>Status: Operational
Owner/Operator: INEOS FPS
Role: Crude buffer between the Forties Pipeline System and the Hound Point jetty — decouples continuous pipeline flow from batch tanker liftings
Storage capacity: Six floating-roof tanks, ~3 million barrels; fed from Kinneil by a 19 km, 30-inch line; linked to Hound Point by 48- and 40-inch loading lines (~5 km)
Handles: Stabilised Forties Blend
Risks: Modest storage against flows of several hundred thousand b/d — limited slack if liftings are delayed</description>
					<styleUrl>#msn_red-circle0</styleUrl>
					<Point>
						<coordinates>-3.38,55.99,0</coordinates>
					</Point>
				</Placemark>
				<Placemark id="ADDHPT00000000000001">
					<name>Hound Point Marine Terminal</name>
					<description>Status: Operational
Owner/Operator: INEOS FPS (within the Forth Ports area); opened 1975
Role: Deep-water export terminal — the loading point of Forties Blend (~38–41° API, ~0.6–0.8% sulphur), a Dated Brent benchmark grade
Berths / vessel size: Two deep-water jetties on an offshore island structure, tankers to ~350,000 dwt — one of the few UK terminals able to load VLCCs; ~200 vessels/yr; 8,000 m³/hour per berth; vapour recovery; escort tugs
Connectivity: Wellhead → FPS → Kinneil → Dalmeny → Hound Point → sea
Risks: Benchmark sensitivity — outages feed straight into Dated Brent assessments; Firth weather and tides</description>
					<styleUrl>#msn_red-circle0</styleUrl>
					<Point>
						<coordinates>-3.359,56.005,0</coordinates>
					</Point>
				</Placemark>
				<Placemark id="ADDSIOT0000000000001">
					<name>SIOT Trieste Marine Terminal</name>
					<description>Status: Operational
Owner/Operator: SIOT / TAL Group (OMV, Shell, Eni, ExxonMobil, MERO ČR, Gunvor, TotalEnergies, Phillips 66, Rosneft)
Role: Crude import head of the TAL system — the sea gate of the Alpine corridor to Bavaria (Bayernoil, Gunvor Ingolstadt), Karlsruhe (MiRO), Austria (AWP branch) and Czechia (IKL)
Capacity: ~37 Mt/yr received, ~400 tankers a year
Berths / vessel size: Marine piers with tanker berths — first Suezmax berthed May 2025 after the Fincantieri revamp; tank farm feeding the TAL pipeline
Risks: Corridor concentration — an outage at Trieste ripples into refineries in four countries; Adriatic weather and port constraints</description>
					<styleUrl>#msn_red-circle0</styleUrl>
					<Point>
						<coordinates>13.76228349369942,45.64661359195608,0</coordinates>
					</Point>
				</Placemark>
				<Placemark id="0E3910379A3EAF1571E4">
					<name>Pulau Bukom Oil Storage &amp; Marine Terminal</name>
					<description>Status: Operational
Owner/Operator: Aster Chemicals and Energy
Role: Crude import, storage and product-export terminal of the Bukom refinery — exports fuels and petrochemicals into the Asian trading market
Berths / vessel size: 13 jetties (12 at Bukom Besar, one at Pulau Ular) plus a Single Buoy Mooring; product tankers, Aframax and Suezmax; buoy upgrades intended to take VLCC cargoes of ~2 million barrels
Crude flows: Crude in mainly via the offshore buoy; products move by submarine pipeline and load at the jetties
Recent: Aster upgrading the buoy and berth infrastructure to cut crude-import costs
Risks: Strait congestion; collisions and spills; berth outages; severe thunderstorms; regional shipping disruption</description>
