				<Placemark id="06549B54963E92547180">
					<name>Primorsk oil terminal port</name>
					<description>Status: Operational
Owner/Operator: Transneft (Primorsk Trade Port)
Role: Russia&apos;s largest Baltic export terminal — the marine end of the Baltic Pipeline System (BPS-1), loading Urals crude plus ultra-low-sulphur diesel delivered by the Sever products pipeline
Capacity: ~1.5 million b/d system design; ~700,000 b/d of typical export handling in 2026, split roughly two-thirds crude, one-third diesel
Connectivity: Pipeline-fed (unlike rail-fed Vysotsk next door); cargoes exit via the Gulf of Finland and the Danish Straits inspection zone, largely on the shadow fleet under the G7 price cap
Recent: The primary target of the Ukrainian deep-strike campaign against export revenue — first loading suspension September 2025, renewed strikes through 2026 including the July attack wave on the Leningrad region ports, with fires fought at the terminal
Strategic role: The single most important outlet for seaborne Urals — the western twin of Kozmino, and the asset your BPS-1 line delivers to
Risks: Drone strikes; price-cap and insurance enforcement at the Danish Straits; winter ice; single-corridor concentration with BPS-1</description>
