				</Placemark>
				<Placemark>
					<name>Yanbu crude oil terminal</name>
					<description>Status: Operational
Owner/Operator: Saudi Aramco
Role: The Red Sea export gate of Saudi crude — the marine terminus of the East–West Pipeline (Petroline), loading Arabian grades at the Yanbu South and North terminals without any Hormuz transit
Berths / vessel size: Multiple deep-water berths and sea-island loading up to VLCC/ULCC scale (~500,000 dwt)
Storage capacity: Large crude tank farms buffering Petroline&apos;s ~5 million b/d delivery capacity against tanker schedules
Connectivity: Fed from Abqaiq by Petroline; feeds the YASREF, SAMREF and Aramco Yanbu refineries alongside export liftings; northbound cargoes connect into SUMED at Ain Sokhna
Recent: The pivot of the 2026 Hormuz closure — Saudi loadings shifted here en masse, with Yanbu-to-SUMED flows alone hitting 1.8 million b/d in late July (46% of the terminal&apos;s exports) after the Houthi threat declaration against Saudi ports
Strategic role: The place where Saudi Arabia&apos;s two-coast strategy becomes physical — the only Gulf producer&apos;s terminal that kept scaling while the strait was shut
Risks: Inside the Houthi threat perimeter since July 2026; Bab el-Mandeb insecurity for southbound cargoes; concentration of the kingdom&apos;s crisis exports on one coastal complex</description>
					<LookAt>
						<longitude>38.23821066790051</longitude>
						<latitude>23.96546686287854</latitude>
