			</Folder>
			<Folder>
				<name>Offshore tanker loading terminals (SPM / CALM / SBM)</name>
				<Placemark>
					<name>Midia Marine Terminal (SPM)</name>
					<description>Status: Operational
Owner/Operator: Midia Marine Terminal (KMG International / Rompetrol)
Role: The offshore crude buoy of the Petromidia system — a single-point mooring in deep water off Năvodari, discharging Suezmax-class tankers by subsea pipeline into the refinery&apos;s tank farm
Berths / vessel size: SPM handling tankers far larger than the shallow Midia port could ever berth
Crude flows: Kazakh and other seaborne grades in — the entry point of KMG&apos;s own barrel into its own refinery
Strategic role: The private front door of Romania&apos;s largest refinery — a miniature LOOP logic: when your port is too shallow, move the port offshore
Risks: Black Sea drone and mine environment; single-buoy dependence for the country&apos;s biggest plant; weather windows</description>
					<styleUrl>#msn_marina0</styleUrl>
					<Point>
						<coordinates>28.75,44.33,0</coordinates>
					</Point>
				</Placemark>
				<Placemark>
					<name>LOOP Marine Terminal (offshore)</name>
					<description>Status: Operational
Owner/Operator: LOOP LLC — Marathon Pipe Line, Shell and Valero interests
Role: The Louisiana Offshore Oil Port — the only US port able to handle fully laden VLCCs, via single-point mooring buoys ~30 km off Port Fourchon in open Gulf water
Berths / vessel size: SPM buoys serving VLCC/ULCC-scale tankers; subsea pipeline to shore
Crude flows: Built (1981) to import; since 2018 also loading exports — the buoys learned to pump both ways as the US flipped from importer to exporter
Connectivity: Subsea line to the Clovelly storage hub, thence LOCAP to St. James and the refining row
Strategic role: America&apos;s deep-water valve — the physical answer to the question &quot;how does a ULCC touch the US?&quot;
Risks: Hurricane shut-ins; single-system concentration; competition from new Texas deep-water export projects</description>
					<styleUrl>#msn_marina</styleUrl>
					<Point>
						<coordinates>-90.02500000000001,28.885,0</coordinates>
					</Point>
				</Placemark>
				<Placemark>
					<name>Ras Markaz Floating Terminal (SPM)</name>
					<description>Status: Operational
Owner/Operator: Oman Tank Terminal Company (OTTCO, OQ group)
Role: The marine gate of the Ras Markaz Crude Oil Park — a floating import/export terminal ~7 km offshore on the open Arabian Sea, pumping crude ashore through twin 42-inch pipelines to the tank park and its four main pumping stations
Berths / vessel size: Deep-draft mooring able to receive fully laden VLCCs and ULCCs — deliberately deeper water than the nearby Port of Duqm
Crude flows: Today primarily inbound — Kuwaiti and Omani crude arriving by tanker for the Duqm refinery via the 80 km shore pipeline (operational April 2023); export-capable by design, with cumulative throughput reported at ~17 million tonnes by 2025
Connectivity: Twin subsea lines to the tank park; the planned Nahada–Ras Markaz pipeline would connect it overland to the national system and unlock true second-terminal exports of the Omani Blend
Recent: 2026 — the only VLCC-scale crude terminal in the Gulf region with no strait, canal or blockade in any direction; its storage-plus-mooring offer became the market&apos;s textbook safe haven
Strategic role: The physical option on Omani geography — built before the crisis that proved its thesis
Risks: Open-ocean mooring weather windows; dependence on the tanker shuttle until the overland line exists; phased-buildout economics</description>
					<styleUrl>#msn_marina00</styleUrl>
					<Point>
						<coordinates>57.85,18.98,0</coordinates>
					</Point>
				</Placemark>
				<Placemark>
					<name>Mina al-Fahal Crude Terminal</name>
					<description>Status: Operational
Owner/Operator: PDO / OQ system
Role: Oman&apos;s crude export gate since the 1960s — offshore single-buoy moorings loading the Oman Export Blend off the Muscat headland
Berths / vessel size: SBM loading for VLCC-scale tankers in open water
Storage capacity: Coastal tank farm buffering the Main Oil Line against liftings
Connectivity: Terminus of the MOL; the physical delivery basis of the DME Oman futures contract — one of the two benchmarks pricing Gulf crude eastbound
Recent: 2026 — loaded without interruption while every terminal inside the strait queued for escorts: Omani barrels needed no Hormuz transit, and Oman&apos;s benchmark role gained weight accordingly
Strategic role: The quiet winner&apos;s export gate — geography as market advantage
Risks: Open-anchorage weather; single-terminal concentration for the national blend</description>
					<styleUrl>#msn_marina1</styleUrl>
					<Point>
						<coordinates>58.53999999999999,23.65,0</coordinates>
					</Point>
				</Placemark>
				<Placemark>
					<name>Jask Terminal (Goreh–Jask Pipeline)</name>
					<description>Status: Reduced
Owner/Operator: National Iranian Oil Company (POGC/NIOC project entities)
Role: Iran&apos;s own Hormuz bypass — the ~1,000 km Goreh–Jask pipeline from the Khuzestan system to a terminal and SPM loading point on the Gulf of Oman, seaward of the strait; inaugurated 2021 as strategic insurance
Capacity: ~300,000 b/d design; historically underused, with intermittent loadings and limited storage build-out
Recent: 2026 — the bypass logic blunted by a blockade that covers all Iranian ports, not just the strait; the asset&apos;s existence still shapes negotiation leverage
Strategic role: The Iranian mirror of ADCOP — proof that every Gulf producer drew the same conclusion about Hormuz; unlike the UAE&apos;s version, never scaled to matter
Risks: Underinvestment; blockade coverage; single-line exposure across 1,000 km; Gulf of Oman naval activity</description>
					<styleUrl>#msn_red-circle1</styleUrl>
					<Point>
						<coordinates>57.70000000000001,25.6,0</coordinates>
					</Point>
				</Placemark>
				<Placemark>
					<name>Cnergyico SPM – Khalifa Point (offshore)</name>
					<description>Status: Operational
Role: Pakistan&apos;s only Single Point Mooring — VLCC-capable deep-water crude gateway ~11.5 km offshore, feeding the Mouza Kund refining complex by subsea pipeline</description>
					<styleUrl>#msn_red-circle1</styleUrl>
					<Point>
						<coordinates>66.63,24.8,0</coordinates>
					</Point>
				</Placemark>
				<Placemark>
					<name>Bonny CLP / SPM Offshore Loading</name>
					<description>Status: Operational
Owner/Operator: Shell (SPDC / Renaissance)
Role: The actual offshore loading point for Bonny Light — the water at Bonny Island is too shallow for laden tankers, so ships moor at buoys a few km offshore and load via floating hoses (same mechanism as Es Sider in Libya)
Configuration: One Crude Loading Platform plus three SPMs — SPM1 and SPM2 load crude fed by submarine pipeline from the tank farm; SPM3, further offshore, handles discharge via a pipeline end manifold
Vessels: Suezmax, some VLCC
Risks: Offshore transfer exposure — weather windows, hose and mooring integrity</description>
					<styleUrl>#msn_red-circle1</styleUrl>
					<Point>
						<coordinates>7.2367,4.1867,0</coordinates>
					</Point>
				</Placemark>
				<Placemark>
					<name>Es Sider SBM Offshore Loading Buoys</name>
					<description>Status: Operational
Role: The 2 offshore Single Buoy Moorings where tankers actually moor &amp; load Es Sider crude (~3 km offshore)
Operator: Waha Oil Co / NOC</description>
					<styleUrl>#msn_red-circle1</styleUrl>
					<Point>
						<coordinates>18.356,30.688,0</coordinates>
					</Point>
				</Placemark>
				<Placemark>
					<name>Girassol Export Buoy (CALM)</name>
					<description>Status: Operational
Owner/Operator: TotalEnergies
Role: Offshore CALM loading buoy — tankers of 80,000–400,000 dwt load directly; no pipeline to shore</description>
					<styleUrl>#msn_red-circle1</styleUrl>
