			</Folder>
			<Folder>
				<name>Pipeline head &amp; gathering stations</name>
				<Placemark>
					<name>Superior Terminal (Enbridge)</name>
					<description>Status: Operational
Owner/Operator: Enbridge
Role: The pivot of the Mainline system — the Wisconsin tank farm at the head of Lake Superior where Canadian crude arriving from the prairies is sorted between the system&apos;s US legs
Storage capacity: Millions of barrels of batching and break-out tankage
Connectivity: Mainline in from Hardisty/Edmonton via Gretna and Clearbrook; Line 61 out to Flanagan (the largest-capacity crude pipe in North America); Line 6A out to the Chicago area and Whiting; Line 5 east through Michigan — the politically contested Straits of Mackinac crossing
Strategic role: Where Canada&apos;s export river braids into its American channels — every downstream junction on this map (Flanagan, Patoka, Cushing) receives what Superior dispatches
Risks: System-wide single point; Line 5 shutdown litigation reshaping flows; winter operations</description>
					<styleUrl>#msn_red-circle10</styleUrl>
					<Point>
						<coordinates>-92.06162531085339,46.68786930884374,0</coordinates>
					</Point>
				</Placemark>
				<Placemark>
					<name>Flanagan Terminal (Enbridge)</name>
					<description>Status: Operational
Owner/Operator: Enbridge
Role: The Illinois junction the system turns on — a tank farm outside a village of a thousand people where Line 61 ends and two southbound choices begin
Storage capacity: Large break-out tankage for batching between systems
Connectivity: Line 61 in from Superior; Flanagan South out to Cushing (the WTI hub); the Southern Access Extension out to Patoka (the Capline corridor); a spur toward the Chicago refineries
Strategic role: The decision point of the Canadian barrel — from this field in Illinois it either becomes Cushing inventory priced against WTI or Patoka throughput bound for Louisiana; the junction is rural because that is exactly where junctions belong
Risks: Junction concentration; apportionment upstream deciding what arrives at all</description>
					<styleUrl>#msn_red-circle10</styleUrl>
					<Point>
						<coordinates>-88.86,40.88,0</coordinates>
					</Point>
				</Placemark>
				<Placemark>
					<name>Patoka Oil Terminal Hub</name>
					<description>Status: Operational
Owner/Operator: Multiple — Marathon, Energy Transfer and partner tank farms around one Illinois village
Role: The crude crossroads of the Midwest — the junction where Bakken barrels (Dakota Access), Canadian barrels (Enbridge via the Southern Access Extension) and Cushing-system barrels (Ozark/Woodpat via Wood River) meet several million barrels of tankage
Connectivity: Two southbound exits compete for its crude — the reversed Capline to St. James, Louisiana, and Energy Transfer&apos;s ETCOP line to Nederland, Texas; local pipes feed the Robinson and Wood River refineries
Strategic role: The quiet answer to &quot;where do Canada, the Bakken and Cushing actually meet&quot; — a hub whose tanks decide whether northern crude becomes Gulf export, Louisiana refinery feed or Midwest gasoline
Risks: Junction concentration; utilisation competition between its two Gulf exits; tornado-belt weather</description>
					<styleUrl>#msn_red-circle9</styleUrl>
					<Point>
						<coordinates>-89.07234831493921,38.79079123080378,0</coordinates>
					</Point>
				</Placemark>
				<Placemark>
					<name>St. James Crude Terminal Hub</name>
					<description>Status: Operational
Owner/Operator: Multiple terminal operators (Plains, NuStar/Sunoco and others) around one river bend in St. James Parish
Role: Louisiana&apos;s onshore crude crossroads — the tank-farm hub where river barges, ocean tankers, Capline, LOCAP from the LOOP system and local pipelines interconnect
Benchmark connection: Light Louisiana Sweet (LLS) — the US Gulf&apos;s light sweet reference grade — is priced basis St. James, making this bend of the river a pricing point in its own right
Connectivity: LOCAP from Clovelly/LOOP; Capline northbound-turned-southbound; river docks; refinery feeds up and down the bank
Strategic role: What Cushing is to WTI, St. James is to LLS — the smaller, saltwater-connected sibling among US pricing locations
Risks: Hurricane and flood exposure; terminal concentration on one reach of river; competition from Texas export hubs</description>
					<styleUrl>#msn_red-circle8</styleUrl>
					<Point>
						<coordinates>-90.85332413635959,30.01193579286889,0</coordinates>
					</Point>
				</Placemark>
				<Placemark>
					<name>Saih Nihayda Blending &amp; Gathering Hub</name>
					<description>Status: Operational
Owner/Operator: Petroleum Development Oman
Role: The midstream heart of the southern trunk — gathering, processing and custody point where southern crude streams are collected before joining the northern flow at the Nahada junction; project documents name it as the collection-and-mixing node of the Oman Blend&apos;s southern half
Connectivity: On the southern trunk from Marmul/Qarn Alam; ~130 km south of the Main Oil Line junction at Nahada, where southern and northern crudes physically merge for the run to Mina al-Fahal; designated tie-in of the planned Nahada–Ras Markaz export line
Recent: Also a major PDO gas hub — the Saih Nihayda gas plant processes gas and condensate from the central-Oman gas fields, alongside the Saih Rawl complex feeding Oman LNG
Strategic role: The node that decides what the southern half of a benchmark grade looks like — blend quality is made here and finalized in the Fahal tanks
Risks: Single-node concentration for southern evacuation; gas-oil facility interdependence on one site</description>
					<styleUrl>#msn_red-circle2</styleUrl>
					<Point>
						<coordinates>56.71353192278404,21.34296396673012,0</coordinates>
					</Point>
				</Placemark>
				<Placemark>
					<name>Fao Storage &amp; Export Tank Farm</name>
					<description>Status: Operational
Owner/Operator: Basra Oil Company / Iraqi Oil Ministry
Role: The staging tank farm of Iraqi exports on the Al-Faw peninsula — southern crude converges here before the subsea lines to the Al-Basra Oil Terminal and SPM buoys
Storage capacity: The buffer between steady field production and tanker liftings — and the system&apos;s proven pressure point
Connectivity: Trunklines from Rumaila, West Qurna, Zubair and Majnoon; subsea export lines to ABOT; the planned Basra–Haditha line would branch from this system
Recent: March 2026 — storage saturated within days of the Hormuz closure, forcing field shut-ins across the south: the textbook demonstration that storage depth, not production capacity, set Iraq&apos;s crisis output
Strategic role: Iraq&apos;s Fao is what Kuwait&apos;s Ahmadi and Saudi Abqaiq are — except with no second exit behind it
Risks: Saturation with nowhere to divert; single-peninsula concentration; naval and drone exposure at the head of the Gulf</description>
					<styleUrl>#msn_red-circle00</styleUrl>
					<Point>
						<coordinates>48.44,29.96,0</coordinates>
					</Point>
				</Placemark>
				<Placemark>
					<name>Habshan Processing &amp; Gathering Complex</name>
					<description>Status: Operational
Owner/Operator: ADNOC
Role: The hinge of the UAE&apos;s onshore oil and gas system — gathering, processing and stabilisation for the Murban crude stream (Bab and the wider onshore fields) plus major gas processing trains (Habshan-Bab complex)
Connectivity: The origin of the Abu Dhabi Crude Oil Pipeline (~1.5 million b/d design) across the Hajar mountains to Fujairah — the pipe that lets Murban ignore the Strait of Hormuz
Recent: 2026 — the busiest node in the Gulf&apos;s most successful chokepoint workaround; the UAE held 82% of its 2025 export rate through the closure largely because this complex feeds a non-Hormuz outlet
Strategic role: The UAE&apos;s Abqaiq — the processing hinge every onshore barrel passes through, and the reason Fujairah works
Risks: Single-complex concentration for the Murban system; industrial and drone exposure; gas-oil interdependence on one site</description>
					<styleUrl>#msn_ylw-pushpin</styleUrl>
					<Point>
						<coordinates>53.61610776152907,23.82631426915676,0</coordinates>
					</Point>
				</Placemark>
				<Placemark>
					<name>Abqaiq Processing Complex</name>
					<description>Status: Operational
Owner/Operator: Saudi Aramco
Role: The world&apos;s largest crude oil stabilisation and processing facility (~7 million b/d capacity) — Ghawar, Khurais and other fields&apos; crude is stabilised, sweetened and batched here before export
Connectivity: The hinge of the entire Saudi system — eastward to the Ras Tanura / Ju&apos;aymah terminals, westward as the origin of the Petroline to Yanbu; spheroids, stabilisation columns and tank farm on site
Recent: The 14 September 2019 drone-and-missile attack halved Saudi production (~5.7 million b/d offline) in one strike — the defining proof that processing nodes, not fields, are the system&apos;s true chokepoints; central to 2026 westbound rerouting during the Hormuz closure
Risks: Single-point concentration without equal; repeat-attack exposure; every Saudi barrel&apos;s path runs through or around this plant</description>
					<styleUrl>#msn_ylw-pushpin</styleUrl>
					<Point>
						<coordinates>49.66899999999999,25.937,0</coordinates>
					</Point>
				</Placemark>
				<Placemark>
					<name>Haoud El Hamra Gathering &amp; Pump Station</name>
					<description>Status: Operational
Owner/Operator: Sonatrach TRC
Role: Central crude gathering hub of the Algerian Sahara — origin of the northbound export trunklines</description>
					<styleUrl>#msn_ylw-pushpin</styleUrl>
					<Point>
						<coordinates>5.97306,31.88944,0</coordinates>
					</Point>
				</Placemark>
