		<Folder>
			<name>Ports &amp; Logistics Hubs</name>
			<Folder>
				<name>Seaports &amp; energy ports</name>
				<Placemark>
					<name>Port of Constanța &amp; Oil Terminal</name>
					<description>Status: Operational
Owner/Operator: Port of Constanța administration; Oil Terminal SA (state-owned) runs the liquid-bulk berths and ~1.7 million m³ tank farm
Role: The Black Sea&apos;s largest port and Romania&apos;s oil gate — crude imports for the Ploiești refineries, product exports, and since 2022 a logistics lifeline for Ukrainian trade
Berths / vessel size: Deep-water crude and product berths; the largest tankers use the Midia buoy up the coast instead
Connectivity: Head of the Conpet pipeline to Ploiești; rail to the whole refining system
Recent: The 2026 fuel squeeze ran through its berths — &quot;reopened trading routes&quot; for extra crude meant more tankers here
Strategic role: Romania&apos;s answer to every supply question — when refineries stop, imports through Constanța start
Risks: Black Sea war exposure (mines, drones, insurance); congestion with grain and military logistics</description>
					<styleUrl>#msn_ferry0</styleUrl>
					<Point>
						<coordinates>28.64,44.16,0</coordinates>
					</Point>
				</Placemark>
				<Placemark>
					<name>Port of Augusta</name>
					<description>Status: Operational
Owner/Operator: Port System Authority of the Eastern Sicilian Sea; refinery jetties operated by ISAB and Sonatrach; a major Italian naval base shares the bay
Role: One of Italy&apos;s principal oil ports — the natural harbour whose refinery jetties, product berths and anchorage made &quot;CIF Augusta&quot; the Mediterranean&apos;s cargo-pricing shorthand
Berths / vessel size: Deep-water oil jetties for crude and product tankers; sheltered anchorage for waiting cargoes
Connectivity: Feeds and drains the two refineries; no pipeline leaves the island — every barrel arrives and departs by sea, which is precisely why the hub prices cargoes, not pipelines
Strategic role: The harbour that names the benchmark — central Mediterranean geography between Suez, Gibraltar and the Adriatic
Risks: Refinery dependence for its traffic; naval-civilian space sharing; the hub&apos;s fate tracks ISAB&apos;s</description>
					<styleUrl>#msn_ferry</styleUrl>
					<Point>
						<coordinates>15.22,37.22,0</coordinates>
					</Point>
				</Placemark>
				<Placemark>
					<name>Port of South Louisiana</name>
					<description>Status: Operational
Owner/Operator: Port of South Louisiana authority; docks operated by refiners, grain houses and terminal companies
Role: The largest port in the United States by tonnage — a 54-mile jurisdiction along the river between New Orleans and Baton Rouge containing the Garyville and Norco refinery docks, grain elevators and liquid terminals
Berths / vessel size: Dozens of deep-draft berths along the channel (~45-47 ft) — Aframax and partially laden Suezmax tankers, but never a laden VLCC
Connectivity: The meeting point of ocean shipping and the Mississippi barge system that reaches the Midwest — plus every pipeline of the corridor
Strategic role: The port identity of the American Rhine — where the river row&apos;s crude arrives and its products leave; the tonnage gateway, while LOOP offshore remains the crude gateway the draft limit forced outside
Risks: Hurricanes and river flooding; channel dredging dependence; concentration of national trade on one waterway</description>
					<styleUrl>#msn_ferry02</styleUrl>
					<Point>
						<coordinates>-90.56793030901102,30.05348062279684,0</coordinates>
					</Point>
				</Placemark>
				<Placemark>
					<name>Petersburg Oil Terminal port</name>
					<description>Status: Operational
Owner/Operator: Petersburg Oil Terminal (PST) — the largest stevedore of the Big Port of St. Petersburg, in operation since 1995 on a site handling oil since 1892
Role: Products transshipment and bunkering hub — fuel oil, diesel and gasoil arriving by rail (5 million tonnes received in 2025), stored and loaded to tankers and bunker barges
Storage capacity: Multi-berth tank farm developed with ~$270m of investment; rail racks, river and road reception
Recent: Struck in the large-scale July 2026 drone attack on the city — the oil terminal hit, damage contained; also an export route for Belarusian products under the Russia-Belarus port agreement
Strategic role: The urban products port of the Baltic system — smaller than Primorsk and Ust-Luga but the region&apos;s bunkering and rail-fed flexibility node
Risks: Drone-strike exposure inside a city of six million; sanctions and price-cap enforcement on every cargo; winter ice</description>
					<styleUrl>#msn_ylw-pushpin0</styleUrl>
					<Point>
						<coordinates>30.17158086019739,59.8813819450667,0</coordinates>
					</Point>
				</Placemark>
				<Placemark>
					<name>Port of Vysotsk – RPK Lukoil-II Terminal</name>
					<description>Status: Operational
Owner/Operator: The RPK-Vysotsk &quot;Lukoil-II&quot; products terminal, opened 2004; the peninsula also hosts the port&apos;s coal berths and the Novatek/Gazprombank Cryogas-Vysotsk LNG plant (largely idled since 2025)
Role: Rail-fed products export terminal in the Gulf of Finland — fuel oil, gasoil and diesel railed from Russian refineries and loaded for export, unlike pipeline-fed Primorsk next door
Recent: Struck in the July 2026 drone attack on the Leningrad region ports; the adjacent LNG plant burned in a May 2024 fire and has since near-halted output
Strategic role: The rail valve of the Baltic products system — flexible where the pipelines are fixed, and now equally targetable
Risks: Drone exposure; rail-logistics dependence; shadow-fleet loading scrutiny; ice-class navigation season</description>
					<styleUrl>#msn_ylw-pushpin0</styleUrl>
					<Point>
						<coordinates>28.56909151350339,60.59454793134434,0</coordinates>
					</Point>
				</Placemark>
				<Placemark>
					<name>Port of Kaliningrad Oil Terminal</name>
					<description>Status: Operational
Owner/Operator: Kaliningrad commercial seaport with Lukoil&apos;s regional infrastructure — Lukoil-Kaliningradmorneft produces ~20,000 b/d from the exclave&apos;s small onshore and offshore fields, railed to port for export
Role: The exclave&apos;s oil window — modest crude exports out, refined products in for a territory with no land link to Russia through EU space
Recent: 2026 — the exclave&apos;s supply chain runs entirely by sea past NATO coasts and through the Danish Straits inspection zone; every cargo is a sanctions-regime test case
Strategic role: The smallest of Russia&apos;s four Baltic oil ports and the most politically exposed — an energy island inside the EU&apos;s perimeter
Risks: Total seaborne dependence; interdiction and inspection escalation; small scale against big geopolitics</description>
					<styleUrl>#msn_ylw-pushpin0</styleUrl>
					<Point>
						<coordinates>20.41939841377085,54.69055793922843,0</coordinates>
					</Point>
				</Placemark>
				<Placemark>
					<name>Port of Sohar</name>
					<description>Status: Operational
Owner/Operator: Sohar Industrial Port Company — a Sultanate of Oman / Port of Rotterdam joint venture
Role: Oman&apos;s northern industrial port — refinery and petrochemical feedstock intake, product exports, containers, bulk and one of the Gulf of Oman&apos;s main bunkering and anchorage zones
Berths / vessel size: Deep-water berths across liquid, bulk and container terminals
Recent: A 2026 workaround hub — ADNOC&apos;s naphtha moved through Sohar by ship-to-ship transfer to bypass Hormuz, and the anchorage absorbed diverted regional traffic
Strategic role: The Rotterdam-model port on the outside of the strait — industrial depth plus crisis flexibility
Risks: Congestion under diverted flows; industrial-zone incidents; competition from Fujairah next door</description>
					<styleUrl>#msn_ylw-pushpin0</styleUrl>
					<Point>
						<coordinates>56.61442148262615,24.51169630707951,0</coordinates>
					</Point>
				</Placemark>
				<Placemark>
					<name>Port of Duqm</name>
					<description>Status: Operational
Owner/Operator: Port of Duqm Company (Oman–Antwerp partnership) within the Duqm SEZ
Role: The Arabian Sea greenfield — deep-water commercial port, one of the region&apos;s largest dry docks, refinery berths and the gateway of the Duqm industrial project
Berths / vessel size: Deep-water quays serving the refinery, dry dock and general trade; open-ocean access with no strait in any direction
Recent: 2026 elevated Duqm from ambition to argument — the war economy rewarded every berth that faced open water
Strategic role: The Indian Ocean face of the Gulf&apos;s energy system — and with Ras Markaz next door, the outside-Hormuz storage-and-processing cluster the region always said it needed
Risks: Remote hinterland; ramp-up economics; dependence on anchor projects (refinery, dry dock, storage park)</description>
					<styleUrl>#msn_ylw-pushpin0</styleUrl>
					<Point>
						<coordinates>57.71,19.68,0</coordinates>
					</Point>
				</Placemark>
				<Placemark>
					<name>Shuaiba Industrial Port &amp; Oil Berths</name>
					<description>Status: Operational
Owner/Operator: Kuwait Ports Authority industrial port; KNPC oil berths and the Mina Abdullah Sea Island alongside; EQUATE and the petrochemical complex inland
Role: The southern export door of Kuwait&apos;s refining strip — product loadings for Mina Abdullah, petrochemical exports, and industrial cargo for the Shuaiba zone
Berths / vessel size: Oil berths and offshore Sea Island loading product tankers up to large scale
Connectivity: Pipeline-fed from the Mina Abdullah complex; the closed Shuaiba refinery site (shut 2017) is being repurposed within the industrial area
Recent: 2026 — throttled with the rest of Kuwaiti exports by the Hormuz closure; the strip from Ahmadi to Al Zour operated as one constrained system
Strategic role: Completes Kuwait&apos;s three-door export coast — Ahmadi, Shuaiba/MAB, Al Zour — all inside the same strait
Risks: Hormuz dependence; coastal concentration; industrial-zone incidents</description>
					<styleUrl>#msn_ylw-pushpin0</styleUrl>
					<Point>
						<coordinates>48.17,29.03,0</coordinates>
					</Point>
				</Placemark>
				<Placemark>
					<name>King Fahd Industrial Port, Jubail</name>
					<description>Status: Operational
Owner/Operator: Saudi Ports Authority; industrial berths serving SATORP, SASREF, SABIC and the Jubail complex
Role: The Gulf-coast industrial port of the world&apos;s largest petrochemical city — crude and feedstock intake, product and chemical exports, project cargo
Berths / vessel size: Deep-water berths for tankers, chemical carriers and bulk vessels across dedicated terminals
Connectivity: Pipeline-fed from the Aramco eastern system; road/rail into the Jubail industrial zones
Recent: 2026 — Gulf-side loadings constrained by the Hormuz closure, the mirror image of Yanbu&apos;s surge on the other coast
Strategic role: The export mouth of Jubail — where the eastern refining-petrochemical cluster meets water, inside the strait
Risks: Hormuz dependence; industrial-zone concentration; regional escalation</description>
					<styleUrl>#msn_ylw-pushpin0</styleUrl>
					<Point>
						<coordinates>49.68000000000001,27.05,0</coordinates>
					</Point>
				</Placemark>
				<Placemark>
					<name>Jeddah Islamic Port &amp; Fuel Terminal</name>
					<description>Status: Operational
Owner/Operator: Saudi Ports Authority; container terminals under concession; fuel storage at the former Jeddah refinery site
Role: Saudi Arabia&apos;s principal commercial port on the Red Sea and the western fuel-distribution hub — the old Jeddah refinery, converted to a storage and distribution terminal, feeds the Makkah-Jeddah region
Connectivity: The western node of the trans-peninsula land bridge — in 2026, cargo rerouted overland from the Gulf moved through Jeddah when Bab el-Mandeb and Hormuz both squeezed
Recent: The &quot;Jeddah land bridge&quot; became a live workaround during the 2026 crisis, moving cargo between coasts by road as both maritime doors jammed
Strategic role: The commercial counterpart of Yanbu&apos;s energy role on the same coast — and the proof that Saudi geography, with two seas, is itself a strategic asset
Risks: Red Sea shipping insecurity; container congestion; dependence of the west on land-bridge capacity when the Gulf closes</description>
					<styleUrl>#msn_ylw-pushpin0</styleUrl>
					<Point>
						<coordinates>39.17,21.47,0</coordinates>
					</Point>
				</Placemark>
				<Placemark>
					<name>Port of Umm Qasr</name>
					<description>Status: Operational
Owner/Operator: General Company for Ports of Iraq; berths run by private operators — ICTSI&apos;s Basra Gateway Terminal (containers, general and oil-sector project cargo), Gulftainer, BMT, CMA-CGM and others
Role: Iraq&apos;s largest and only deep-water commercial port, on the western edge of the Fao peninsula — the country&apos;s main gateway for imports including refined products, food and project equipment for the southern oilfields
Berths / vessel size: 21+ berths across North and South ports; container vessels to 14,000 TEU at the deepest berths (~14 m)
Connectivity: Road and rail to Basra and Baghdad; adjacent Khor al-Zubair port and fuel-storage zone
Recent: Iraq&apos;s product-import dependence made the port a pressure point in the 2026 crisis alongside the crude-export collapse
Strategic role: The non-oil half of Iraq&apos;s sea access — and the import valve for the fuels its refineries don&apos;t yet produce
Risks: Channel and draft limits; congestion; regional escalation at the head of the Gulf</description>
					<styleUrl>#msn_ylw-pushpin0</styleUrl>
					<Point>
						<coordinates>47.93,30.05,0</coordinates>
					</Point>
				</Placemark>
				<Placemark>
					<name>Port Qasim Energy Complex (FOTCO / WOTS-I)</name>
					<description>Status: Operational
Role: The modern import gate — FOTCO jetty and OMC terminals feeding PAPCO&apos;s White Oil Terminal Station, head of the White Oil Pipeline</description>
					<styleUrl>#msn_ylw-pushpin0</styleUrl>
					<Point>
						<coordinates>67.34211143442742,24.77864165400217,0</coordinates>
					</Point>
				</Placemark>
				<Placemark>
					<name>Keamari Oil Piers &amp; Terminals – Karachi Port</name>
					<description>Status: Operational
Role: Pakistan&apos;s historic import gate — KPT oil piers, crude berthing for PRL, NRL harbour storage, PSO terminals; linked into the pipeline spine via KPLP</description>
