			<Folder>
				<name>Inland ports</name>
				<Placemark>
					<name>Ukrainian Danube Ports (Izmail &amp; Reni)</name>
					<description>Status: Reduced
Owner/Operator: Ukrainian port authorities — the river ports of the Danube delta&apos;s north bank, directly across from Romanian territory
Role: Ukraine&apos;s wartime lifeline — when the Black Sea ports were blockaded, Izmail and Reni became the export escape route, with cargo transferred to the Sulina channel and Constanța under NATO&apos;s shore
Handles: Grain outbound above all; fuel and supplies inbound — the products that keep the south of a country at war moving
Recent: Repeatedly struck by drones since 2023 — attacks landing within sight of Romanian (NATO) territory, with debris crossing the river; traffic continues under fire
Strategic role: The war&apos;s Danube front on this map — the reason the canal, Constanța and the whole Romanian corridor acquired strategic weight far beyond oil
Risks: Ongoing strikes; insurance and crewing under fire; escalation across a NATO border river</description>
					<styleUrl>#default</styleUrl>
					<Point>
						<coordinates>28.84809737560538,45.32126055418616,0</coordinates>
					</Point>
				</Placemark>
				<Placemark>
					<name>Giurgiulești International Free Port (Moldova)</name>
					<description>Status: Operational
Owner/Operator: Moldova&apos;s only port, held via international investors and the EBRD&apos;s involvement — on the country&apos;s ~430 m of Danube frontage where Moldova, Romania and Ukraine meet
Role: The intake valve of a fuel-import-dependent nation — oil products terminal, tank farm and berths supplying a country with zero refineries of its own
Connectivity: Danube barge and river-sea vessels; road and rail into Moldova; Romanian refinery products dominate the supply
Strategic role: The end of the Romanian value chain across the border — Petromidia&apos;s and Petrobrazi&apos;s barrels become Moldova&apos;s energy security here; the map&apos;s clearest case of one nation&apos;s downstream being another&apos;s lifeline
Risks: Single-port national dependence; war next door (Ukrainian Danube ports are drone targets kilometres away); Transnistria and supply politics</description>
					<styleUrl>#default</styleUrl>
					<Point>
						<coordinates>28.2,45.47,0</coordinates>
					</Point>
				</Placemark>
				<Placemark>
					<name>Duisburg Inland Port</name>
					<description>Status: Operational
Owner/Operator: Duisburger Hafen AG (duisport) — State of North Rhine-Westphalia and City of Duisburg; fuel and chemical terminals privately run
Role: Europe&apos;s largest inland port — the pivot of the Rhine barge system at the Rhine–Ruhr confluence; refined products and chemicals, coal, ore, steel and containers; RMR tank farm at Homberg
Connectivity: The point where the deep Lower Rhine meets the shallower Middle Rhine — vessels loaded to ~1,200 t must lighten to ~460 t (300–400 t in August 2026) to pass Kaub; rail to the Ruhr and to Rotterdam/Antwerp
Recent: August 2026 — loading capacities down to about one third of normal; freight up to ~€200/tonne (previous record €130/t, August 2022); cargo shifting to rail
Strategic role: The reference point of Rhine freight economics — &quot;above Duisburg&quot; and &quot;below Duisburg&quot; are two different barge markets; the last major hub reachable at near-normal loads when Kaub bites
Risks: Low-water exposure downstream of the chokepoint; steel-industry decline in its hinterland; scarce rail capacity when barges fail</description>
