			<Folder>
				<name>STS / anchorage transfer zones</name>
				<Placemark>
					<name>Hurd&apos;s Bank STS &amp; Bunkering Anchorage (offshore Malta)</name>
					<description>Status: Operational
Owner/Operator: No operator — a shallow bank in international waters east of Malta, used as an anchorage by tankers, bunker suppliers and floating storage
Role: The Mediterranean&apos;s open-sea marketplace — ship-to-ship transfers, bunkering and cargoes waiting for orders just outside territorial jurisdiction, in the middle of the Suez–Gibraltar lane
Recent: The sanctions era made it a watchword — Russian-linked product transfers, blending and storage-afloat episodes drew enforcement attention; the third specimen of this map&apos;s STS family with Port Said and North Larak
Strategic role: Where Mediterranean cargoes change hands, identities and destinations at anchor — the informal half of the pricing zone whose formal half is Augusta
Risks: Jurisdictional grey zone by design; collision and spill exposure among anchored tankers; escalating scrutiny of exactly these waters</description>
					<styleUrl>#msn_ferry</styleUrl>
					<Point>
						<coordinates>14.75,35.95,0</coordinates>
					</Point>
				</Placemark>
				<Placemark>
					<name>North Larak Anchorage – STS Transfer Zone</name>
					<description>Status: Operational
Owner/Operator: No formal operator — an anchorage off Larak Island inside the strait, used by the Iranian and shadow-fleet tanker system
Role: Iran&apos;s sanctions-era transfer zone — ship-to-ship operations, floating storage and cargo identity-laundering at anchor, the Gulf counterpart of the Port Said STS zone on this map
Recent: August 2026 — ~35 tanker and cargo hulls recorded, roughly 74% transmitting no AIS (&quot;dark&quot;), including sanctioned vessels, as Iran rerouted flows around the blockaded Kharg system; Caspian Russia-to-Iran shipments nearly tripled in parallel
Strategic role: Where blocked barrels change hands — the visible signature of an export system operating outside the formal market
Risks: Collisions and spills among dark, ageing tankers; enforcement action; the anchorage is itself inside the contested strait</description>
					<styleUrl>#msn_ferry1</styleUrl>
					<Point>
						<coordinates>56.41484311300043,26.88008478563453,0</coordinates>
					</Point>
				</Placemark>
				<Placemark>
					<name>Port Said Anchorage - STS Transfer Zone</name>
					<description>Status: Operational
Owner/Operator: No single operator — a designated anchorage managed by Egyptian port and canal authorities
Role: Offshore ship-to-ship transfer zone at the Suez Canal&apos;s northern entrance — no fixed infrastructure; tankers moor side by side with fenders and pump cargo across at anchor
Recent: One of the main STS hubs of the sanctions era — naphtha loaded at Russia&apos;s Ust-Luga transferred here (and at Al Hoceima, Morocco) before onward delivery to Asia, with monthly volumes above 200,000 t; scarce ice-class tankers shuttle short runs out of Russian ports and hand cargoes to ordinary tankers mid-Mediterranean
Strategic role: Where sanctioned barrels change identity — hundreds of ships waiting for canal convoys make two tankers alongside unremarkable
Risks: Cargo origin becomes hard to trace (the point for some users, a compliance nightmare for others); ageing shadow-fleet tankers with unclear insurance — a spill could go uncompensated; growing Western scrutiny of exactly these zones</description>
