	<Folder>
		<name>C. Corridors</name>
		<Folder>
			<name>Crude Pipelines</name>
			<Folder>
				<name>Trunk lines</name>
				<Placemark>
					<name>Conpet Crude Pipeline (Constanța–Ploiești)</name>
					<description>Status: Operational
Route: The Constanța import terminals → west across the Danube plain via Cernavodă and Urziceni → the Ploiești refining cluster
Owner/Operator: Conpet SA — Romania&apos;s state pipeline carrier, which also gathers the domestic onshore fields
Handles: Imported seaborne crude inland to Petrobrazi and (when it runs) Petrotel; domestic crude within the same national network
Strategic role: The line that connects the sea to the birthplace — imported barrels travelling to the town where refining was invented; its Petrotel deliveries stopped with the sanctions freeze
Risks: Ageing state infrastructure; throughput collapse when a customer refinery dies; tariff dependence on few shippers</description>
					<styleUrl>#op4</styleUrl>
					<LineString>
						<tessellate>1</tessellate>
						<coordinates>
							28.64,44.17,0 28.03,44.34,0 27.5,44.45,0 26.63,44.72,0 26.08,44.94,0 
						</coordinates>
					</LineString>
				</Placemark>
				<Placemark>
					<name>Capline Pipeline (Patoka–St. James, reversed)</name>
					<description>Status: Operational
Route: Patoka, Illinois → down the Mississippi valley past Memphis → St. James, Louisiana
Owner/Operator: Marathon-led ownership (operated by Marathon Pipe Line)
Capacity: Historically up to ~1.2 million b/d northbound; reversed and restarted southbound in 2021–22 at lower initial rates
Handles: Canadian heavy and Bakken barrels flowing SOUTH to the Gulf — the exact opposite of its 1968 design, which carried imported crude north to Midwest refineries
Recent: The reversal is the quiet twin of Seaway&apos;s — the second great US artery to flip direction as domestic production replaced imports; it re-linked the Hardisty/Cushing world to the Louisiana refining row
Strategic role: The Mississippi valley&apos;s crude spine — its flow direction is a one-line history of US oil
Risks: Utilisation ramp economics; competition with other heavy-crude routes; river-crossing integrity</description>
					<styleUrl>#op30</styleUrl>
					<LineString>
						<tessellate>1</tessellate>
						<coordinates>
							-89.07291234364733,38.78943007760477,0 -89.5,37.2,0 -90,35.15,0 -90.5,33.5,0 -91,31.8,0 -90.95,30.6,0 -90.85332983881489,30.01717044464003,0 
						</coordinates>
					</LineString>
				</Placemark>
				<Placemark>
					<name>LOCAP Pipeline (Clovelly–St. James)</name>
					<description>Status: Operational
Route: The Clovelly storage hub → ~90 km north → the St. James terminal complex
Owner/Operator: LOCAP LLC (LOOP-affiliated)
Capacity: Sized for the LOOP system&apos;s full throughput
Handles: Imported and offshore-Gulf crude from the LOOP caverns into the onshore hub, refineries and Capline interconnection — and export barrels the other way
Strategic role: The short pipe that connects America&apos;s only VLCC port to its Louisiana pricing point — the last link between a supertanker at sea and an LLS quote at St. James
Risks: Single-corridor dependence for the LOOP system; wetland routing and integrity</description>
					<styleUrl>#op30</styleUrl>
					<LineString>
						<tessellate>1</tessellate>
						<coordinates>
							-90.27016399746073,29.44878916472324,0 -90.65000000000001,29.82,0 -90.85124832836425,30.01595479611584,0 
						</coordinates>
					</LineString>
				</Placemark>
				<Placemark>
					<name>Enbridge Southern Access Extension (Flanagan–Patoka)</name>
					<description>Status: Operational
Route: The Flanagan terminal in central Illinois — the southern end of the Enbridge Mainline system arriving from Superior, Wisconsin and ultimately from Edmonton/Hardisty — south to Patoka
Owner/Operator: Enbridge
Capacity: ~300,000 b/d
Handles: Canadian light, synthetic and heavy grades — the Hardisty barrel&apos;s route into the Capline corridor
Connectivity: At Flanagan the Mainline also launches Flanagan South toward Cushing — one junction, two southbound choices
Strategic role: The link that lets Western Canadian Select reach St. James and the Louisiana refining row — Hardisty to the Mississippi in one connected system on this map
Risks: Mainline apportionment upstream; competition with Flanagan South and ETCOP for the same barrels</description>
					<styleUrl>#op30</styleUrl>
					<LineString>
						<tessellate>1</tessellate>
						<coordinates>
							-88.86,40.88,0 -89,39.9,0 -89.07088970921912,38.79082776714489,0 
						</coordinates>
					</LineString>
				</Placemark>
				<Placemark>
					<name>Flanagan South Pipeline (Flanagan–Cushing)</name>
					<description>Status: Operational
Route: Flanagan, Illinois → southwest across Missouri and Kansas → Cushing, Oklahoma
Owner/Operator: Enbridge
Capacity: ~585,000 b/d
Handles: Canadian heavy and light from the Enbridge Mainline into the WTI hub&apos;s tanks — one of Cushing&apos;s principal inbound lines
Strategic role: The second Canadian route into Cushing (beside Keystone) — the reason the benchmark&apos;s tanks hold Alberta barrels, and the western leg of the Flanagan junction&apos;s two-way choice
Risks: Mainline dependence upstream; heavy-light quality management in shared tanks; competition with the Patoka route for Canadian flows</description>
					<styleUrl>#op30</styleUrl>
					<LineString>
						<tessellate>1</tessellate>
						<coordinates>
							-88.85517702816225,40.87327947575499,0 -92,39.5,0 -94.5,37.8,0 -96,36.6,0 -96.76999999999998,35.95,0 
						</coordinates>
					</LineString>
				</Placemark>
				<Placemark>
					<name>Ozark Pipeline (Cushing–Wood River)</name>
					<description>Status: Operational
Route: Cushing, Oklahoma → northeast across Missouri → the Wood River terminal and refinery complex on the Mississippi opposite St. Louis
Owner/Operator: Enbridge
Capacity: ~345,000 b/d after expansion
Handles: Cushing-stored crude — WTI-quality light and Canadian grades — to Midwest refining
Strategic role: The benchmark&apos;s eastern outlet — with the short Woodpat link onward it connects Cushing&apos;s tanks to the Patoka hub and thus, since the reversal, to Capline itself: a barrel can now run Cushing → Wood River → Patoka → St. James entirely by pipe
Risks: Utilisation competition; refinery-outage demand swings at Wood River</description>
					<styleUrl>#op30</styleUrl>
					<LineString>
						<tessellate>1</tessellate>
						<coordinates>
							-96.76999999999998,35.95,0 -94.5,37.2,0 -92,38.3,0 -90.06999999999999,38.84,0 
						</coordinates>
					</LineString>
				</Placemark>
				<Placemark>
					<name>Woodpat Pipeline (Wood River–Patoka)</name>
					<description>Status: Operational
Route: The Wood River terminal complex → ~80 km east → the Patoka hub
Owner/Operator: Marathon Pipe Line
Handles: Crude interchange between the two Illinois hubs — the short link that stitches the Cushing/Ozark system into the Patoka junction
Strategic role: Eighty kilometres that connect two worlds — WTI-priced barrels from Oklahoma meeting Bakken and Canadian streams at Patoka
Risks: Small-line dependence for a big interconnection role</description>
					<styleUrl>#op30</styleUrl>
					<LineString>
						<tessellate>1</tessellate>
						<coordinates>
							-90.06999999999999,38.84,0 -89.5,38.8,0 -89.07088970921912,38.79082776714489,0 
						</coordinates>
					</LineString>
				</Placemark>
				<Placemark>
					<name>Enbridge Mainline (Hardisty–Superior, simplified)</name>
					<description>Status: Operational
Route: The Hardisty and Edmonton hubs → east across Saskatchewan past Regina → Cromer, Manitoba → the Gretna border crossing → Clearbrook, Minnesota → Superior, Wisconsin; drawn here as one simplified corridor representing the parallel Lines 1–4
Owner/Operator: Enbridge
Capacity: ~3 million b/d across the parallel lines — the largest crude system on the continent, carrying the majority of all Canadian exports
Handles: The full Western Canadian slate — heavy blends like WCS, synthetics, lights and condensate, batched in sequence down the same pipes
Strategic role: Canada&apos;s economic aorta — the counterpart of Keystone one corridor east, and the upstream explanation of every Enbridge junction on this map
Risks: Apportionment when supply exceeds space; regulatory and treaty exposure at crossings; the system&apos;s sheer indispensability</description>
					<styleUrl>#op30</styleUrl>
					<LineString>
						<tessellate>1</tessellate>
						<coordinates>
							-111.3,52.66999999999999,0 -108,51.5,0 -105,50.5,0 -101,49.7,0 -97.55,48.99999999999999,0 -95.40000000000001,47.5,0 -92.06120648834336,46.68923194191785,0 
						</coordinates>
					</LineString>
				</Placemark>
				<Placemark>
					<name>Enbridge Line 61 (Superior–Flanagan)</name>
					<description>Status: Operational
Route: Superior, Wisconsin → south through Wisconsin and northern Illinois → the Flanagan terminal
Owner/Operator: Enbridge
Capacity: ~1.2 million b/d — the highest-capacity crude oil pipeline in North America
Handles: The Mainline slate southbound — the trunk that feeds both Flanagan South (to Cushing) and the Southern Access Extension (to Patoka)
Strategic role: The busiest single crude pipe on the continent, and almost nobody has heard of it — the definition of invisible criticality
Risks: Concentration of Canadian export flow in one line; expansion permitting battles fought over decades</description>
					<styleUrl>#op30</styleUrl>
					<LineString>
						<tessellate>1</tessellate>
						<coordinates>
							-92.06121760300834,46.68898219291104,0 -91,44.5,0 -89.8,42.5,0 -88.86,40.88,0 
						</coordinates>
					</LineString>
				</Placemark>
				<Placemark>
					<name>Enbridge Line 6A (Superior–Chicago/Whiting)</name>
					<description>Status: Operational
Route: Superior, Wisconsin → southeast through Wisconsin → the Griffith/Hartsdale terminal area of northwest Indiana, serving the Chicago refining belt and BP Whiting
Owner/Operator: Enbridge
Capacity: ~670,000 b/d
Handles: Canadian heavy and light for the Great Lakes refineries — the supply line of the Midwest&apos;s largest plant
Strategic role: The answer to how Whiting eats — the refinery on the lake is fed from the tank farm in the north woods, not from any local source
Risks: Urban-corridor routing at the Chicago end; the 2010 Line 6B Marshall spill on the sister line remains the reference accident of the system</description>
					<styleUrl>#op30</styleUrl>
					<LineString>
						<tessellate>1</tessellate>
						<coordinates>
							-92.06104415328977,46.68885242217053,0 -89.5,44.5,0 -88.2,42.8,0 -87.48595895543127,41.66412845114538,0 
						</coordinates>
					</LineString>
				</Placemark>
				<Placemark>
					<name>Seaway Pipeline (Cushing–Freeport)</name>
					<description>Status: Operational
Route: Cushing → east Texas → Jones Creek terminal at Freeport, with distribution into the Houston refining system
Owner/Operator: Seaway Crude Pipeline — Enterprise Products / Enbridge 50-50
Capacity: ~850,000 b/d across the twinned system
Handles: Cushing-stored crude southbound to Gulf refineries and export docks
Recent: The 2012 flow reversal is the hub&apos;s defining modern event — built to carry imports north, turned around to drain the shale glut south; it re-priced WTI against Brent and created the Cushing–Gulf spread trade
Strategic role: The main southbound valve of the benchmark — when Seaway and Marketlink run full, Cushing drains and WTI firms
Risks: Tariff and capacity competition; leak incidents; dependence of hub balance on two southbound systems</description>
					<styleUrl>#op30</styleUrl>
					<LineString>
						<tessellate>1</tessellate>
						<coordinates>
							-96.76999999999998,35.95,0 -96.59999999999999,34.8,0 -96.40000000000001,33.6,0 -96.30000000000001,32.4,0 -96.40000000000001,31.3,0 -95.90000000000001,30.2,0 -95.34999999999999,28.95,0 
						</coordinates>
					</LineString>
				</Placemark>
				<Placemark>
					<name>TC Marketlink Pipeline (Cushing–Nederland)</name>
					<description>Status: Operational
Route: Cushing → southeast Texas → the Nederland terminal in the Port Arthur–Beaumont refining complex
Owner/Operator: TC Energy (the Gulf Coast leg of the Keystone system)
Capacity: ~700,000+ b/d
Handles: Cushing barrels — including Canadian heavy delivered by Keystone — to the largest US refining concentration
Strategic role: The second southbound valve, pairing with Seaway to connect the paper hub to Gulf physical demand; the reason Canadian crude priced at Hardisty can end up in a Port Arthur coker
Risks: Shared-system exposure with Keystone upstream; tariff competition with Seaway; Gulf Coast demand cycles</description>
