						</coordinates>
					</LineString>
				</Placemark>
				<Placemark>
					<name>Keystone Mainline (Hardisty–Steele City)</name>
					<description>Status: Operational
Route: Hardisty, Alberta → east past Regina → southeast across Manitoba → entering the US in eastern North Dakota → south through the Dakotas and Nebraska → Steele City junction, where the Cushing Extension continues south and an eastern leg runs to Wood River/Patoka, Illinois
Owner/Operator: TC Energy
Capacity: ~590,000+ b/d of Canadian heavy and synthetic crude
Handles: Western Canadian Select and synthetic grades from the Hardisty blending hub toward US Midwest refineries and the Cushing system
Recent: April 2025 — rupture at Fort Ransom, North Dakota (~3,500 barrels), another entry in the system&apos;s spill record after the 2022 Kansas rupture; the Keystone XL shortcut (Hardisty–Steele City direct) was cancelled in 2021, leaving this the system&apos;s only northern leg
Strategic role: Canada&apos;s main southbound crude artery before TMX — the pipe that ties Alberta&apos;s discount barrel to Oklahoma&apos;s benchmark
Risks: Spill history and regulatory scrutiny; single-mainline dependence for the whole system; heavy-crude demand cycles at the far end</description>
					<styleUrl>#op30</styleUrl>
					<LineString>
						<tessellate>1</tessellate>
						<coordinates>
							-111.269128572296,52.64111694635146,0 -108,51.9,0 -104.6,50.3,0 -101.5,49.6,0 -97.8,48.99999999999999,0 -97.5,46.8,0 -97.3,44.5,0 -97.09999999999999,42.5,0 -97.01999999999998,40.05,0 
						</coordinates>
					</LineString>
				</Placemark>
				<Placemark>
					<name>Ceyhan–Kırıkkale Crude Pipeline</name>
					<description>Status: Operational
Route: BOTAŞ Ceyhan terminal → north across the Taurus foothills and central Anatolia → Tüpraş Kırıkkale refinery; the only oil pipeline running inland from the Ceyhan hub
Owner/Operator: BOTAŞ
Handles: Imported and Iraqi-origin crude feeding central Turkey&apos;s refinery — the counterflow to everything else at Ceyhan, which exists to send crude out to sea
Strategic role: The line that connects Turkey&apos;s export hub to its own inland demand — and the answer to why no pipeline reaches Mersin: the state built one crude artery inland, not a products network to the coast
Risks: Single line for the Kırıkkale refinery&apos;s seaborne feedstock; seismic exposure (the 2023 Kahramanmaraş earthquake zone lies on the corridor)</description>
					<styleUrl>#op30</styleUrl>
					<LineString>
