						</coordinates>
					</LineString>
				</Placemark>
				<Placemark>
					<name>MOL Southern Trunk — South Oman to Saih Nihayda</name>
					<description>Status: Operational
Route: The southern production clusters (Marmul, Nimr and the heavy-oil belt, with Mukhaizna feeding in) → north through the Block 6 corridor past Qarn Alam → Saih Nihayda, where the streams are commingled into the Oman Export Blend → junction with the Main Oil Line toward Mina al-Fahal
Owner/Operator: Petroleum Development Oman
Handles: The southern half of Omani production — heavy and medium streams that, blended with the lighter northern crudes, define the Oman Blend quality
Strategic role: The reason Oman has one export system, not two — every southern barrel travels ~800 km north to reach water at Muscat; the geographic inefficiency the planned Ras Markaz link exists to fix
Risks: Single-corridor evacuation for the entire south; long-haul pumping economics; blend-quality management</description>
					<styleUrl>#op30</styleUrl>
					<LineString>
						<tessellate>1</tessellate>
						<coordinates>
							55.18000000000001,18.14,0 55.65,18.65,0 56.09999999999999,19.3,0 56.6,20.2,0 56.67098940868603,20.86703879880407,0 56.7125889725962,21.34342869396917,0 57.51953546021747,22.37978467014029,0 
						</coordinates>
					</LineString>
				</Placemark>
				<Placemark>
					<name>Nahada–Ras Markaz Crude Pipeline (planned)</name>
					<description>Status: Planned
Route: Main Oil Line junction at Nahada → 130 km to Saih Nihayda → 230 km southeast to the Ras Markaz Crude Oil Park; ~360 km of 42-inch line in total
Owner/Operator: OTTCO (OQ group), in coordination with the PDO system
Capacity: To be fixed by the storage-hub study — sized as a second export line for the Omani Blend
Recent: Under conceptual development — the Ras Markaz terminal was completed with no overland connection, so Omani crude for Duqm still travels by coastal tanker from Mina al-Fahal
Strategic role: The line that would complete Oman&apos;s geography — turning Ras Markaz from a sea-fed storage park into a true second export terminal on the open Indian Ocean, and giving the southern fields a southern exit for the first time
Risks: Financing and final investment decision; competition for capital with other OQ projects; the tanker shuttle works well enough to blunt urgency</description>
					<styleUrl>#op30</styleUrl>
					<LineString>
						<tessellate>1</tessellate>
						<coordinates>
							57.52000000000001,22.38,0 56.71407996459693,21.34250836752022,0 57.45000000000001,20.6,0 57.65,19.8,0 
						</coordinates>
					</LineString>
				</Placemark>
				<Placemark>
					<name>Main Oil Line (MOL) — Interior to Mina al-Fahal</name>
					<description>Status: Operational
Route: The interior production clusters (Yibal, Fahud and the gathering network) → Adam → Izki → through the Sumail Gap in the Hajar mountains → Mina al-Fahal terminal, Muscat
Owner/Operator: Petroleum Development Oman
Capacity: Sized for PDO&apos;s full production — the single evacuation spine of Omani crude
Handles: The Oman Export Blend — the commingled stream of light, medium and heavy interior crudes that prices the DME Oman futures contract
Strategic role: The country&apos;s one artery — everything PDO produces crosses the mountains through this line to a coast that lies entirely outside the Strait of Hormuz
Risks: Single-trunkline concentration; mountain-crossing exposure; blend-quality management across dozens of input streams</description>
					<styleUrl>#op30</styleUrl>
					<LineString>
