						</coordinates>
					</LineString>
				</Placemark>
				<Placemark>
					<name>Fishkhabur-Ceyhan pipeline (Turkish leg)</name>
					<description>Status: Operational
Route: Fishkhabur border crossing → Silopi → Mardin → Şanlıurfa → Kahramanmaraş → Ceyhan tank farm and Yumurtalık marine terminal; ~625 km, five pumping stations; in service since 1976
Owner/Operator: BOTAŞ, the Turkish state pipeline company
Capacity: Nameplate up to 1.6 million b/d; currently running at roughly 15%, constrained entirely by upstream supply rather than the pipe
Recent: Shut March 2023 when the ICC ordered Türkiye to pay Iraq ~$1.5bn — the only major line closed by court order rather than conflict; restarted September 2025, resumed March 2026 after a pause. Türkiye terminated the 1973 agreement on 27 July 2026 with a twelve-month wind-down while a replacement framework is negotiated; flows expected to continue through expiry
Strategic role: Iraq&apos;s Hormuz hedge — its only Mediterranean outlet, sharing Ceyhan with BTC and stacking two countries&apos; contingency plans on one coastline
Risks: Treaty vacuum after July 2026 and tariff renegotiation from Turkish strength; southeastern Anatolia security; seismic exposure (the 2023 Kahramanmaraş quake suspended loadings); terminal concentration</description>
					<styleUrl>#op30</styleUrl>
					<LineString>
