				<Placemark id="0A981C3A6A3F2D3C6D14">
					<name>Kirkuk-Fishkhabur Pipeline (federal Iraqi leg)</name>
					<description>Status: Offline
Route: Kirkuk K-1 station → Baiji junction → along the Tigris to Fishkhabur (Turkish border); ~345 km, twin 46&quot;/40&quot; lines (1976/1987); the rebuild reroutes via Salahaddin and Nineveh, deliberately avoiding Kurdistan Region territory
Owner/Operator: Iraqi Oil Ministry via North Oil Company — the Iraqi half of the ITP under the 1973 treaty
Capacity: Historic ITP design up to 1.6 million b/d; the rebuilt Kirkuk–Nineveh segment is specified at 350,000 b/d
Recent: Destroyed in the 2014 ISIS advance and inactive twelve years; hydrostatic testing under way March 2026, with June 2026 targeted for the Kirkuk–Nineveh segment and months of work beyond; no confirmation it carries export barrels — federal crude still routes via the KRI&apos;s Saralo station
Strategic role: Sovereignty restoration — completion ends Baghdad&apos;s physical dependence on a pipe it does not own, converting a hostage situation into an ordinary budget dispute
Risks: Chronic schedule slippage; Nineveh and Salahaddin security; funding and contractor dependence; Kirkuk deliverability after two decades of underinvestment; stranding KEP capacity reopens the Erbil fight</description>
