				<Placemark id="09BE71D1E53F2D4290A0">
					<name>Khurmala-Fishkhabur pipeline (Kurdistan Regional Government&apos;s leg)</name>
					<description>Status: Operational
Route: Khurmala Dome near Erbil → Dohuk → Zakho → Fishkhabur; ~250 km, 40-inch trunk; feeders from Taq Taq, Tawke/Peshkabir and Shaikan/Atrush; federal Kirkuk crude injects at Khurmala
Owner/Operator: KRG; built and operated by KAR Group
Capacity: ~700,000 b/d nameplate; actual throughput roughly a third of that
Recent: Idled from March 2023 by the ICC award; restarted 27 September 2025; halted early 2026 over payments; resumed 18 March 2026. Over 4 million bbl shipped via Ceyhan post-restart; ~190,000 b/d delivered to SOMO, ~50,000 b/d kept for local use; producers net $14/bbl after deductions, so entitlement barrels largely sell domestically at ~$31
Strategic role: The leverage asset — a regional government owns the only functioning link between federal fields and tidewater, which is why the budget dispute has a valve attached
Risks: Militia drone strikes upstream (Tawke installations hit three times in 2025); payment-mechanism instability; single-operator concentration in KAR; obsolescence once the federal line runs</description>
