				<Placemark id="ADDIKL00000000000001">
					<name>TAL extension - IKL pipeline (Ingolstadt–Kralupy–Litvínov)</name>
					<description>Status: Operational
Route: Vohburg/Ingolstadt (junction with TAL) → Czech border → Nelahozeves central tank farm near Kralupy, supplying the Kralupy and Litvínov refineries; 347 km
Owner/Operator: MERO ČR (Czech state pipeline company)
Capacity: ~200,000 b/d usable (~10 Mt/yr design); with TAL-PLUS live since 2025, TAL + IKL can deliver 8 Mt/yr — the entire Czech crude demand
Recent: In March 2025 the Czech Republic completed its switch away from Russian Druzhba deliveries; TAL-PLUS raised TAL throughput from 6,400 to 7,500 m³/hour
Strategic role: The western supply route that made Czech independence from Russian pipeline crude possible — Trieste → Alps → Ingolstadt → Prague region
Risks: Full dependence on the Trieste/TAL corridor now that Druzhba flows have ended; capacity sized close to national demand</description>
					<styleUrl>#op30</styleUrl>
					<LineString>
						<tessellate>1</tessellate>
						<coordinates>
							11.46181430714684,48.80694359842674,0 11.621,48.76800000000001,0 12.35,49.17999999999999,0 12.9,49.50000000000001,0 13.55,49.78,0 14.1,50.08,0 14.31,50.24000000000001,0 14.295,50.26,0 13.61,50.60000000000001,0 
						</coordinates>
					</LineString>
				</Placemark>
				<Placemark>
					<name>TAL extension - Adria-Wien Pipeline (AWP)</name>
					<description>Status: Operational
Route: Branches off the TAL at Würmlach (Carinthia) → Gail, Drau and Jaun valleys → over the Koralpe (highest point 1,350 m) → Styria (Wettmannstätten, Wildon) → over the Wechsel → OMV Schwechat refinery; 420 km, 18-inch, 12 pump stations, ~12,000 m cumulative elevation change; 14 km spur to the Lannach strategic-reserve depot
Owner/Operator: Adria-Wien Pipeline GmbH — OMV ~76% (with BP, Shell, Esso Austria, Agip); commissioned 1970
Capacity: ~8 Mt/yr (up to ~11 Mt technical); crude takes about three days Trieste → Schwechat via TAL + AWP
Recent: The planned Bratislava–Schwechat link to the Druzhba system (BSP, 62 km) was abandoned in March 2022 after 20 years without Slovak permits
Strategic role: Austria&apos;s sole crude artery — the Austrian counterpart of the Bavarian and Czech branches of the same TAL system, all hanging off one Adriatic port
Risks: Single-corridor dependence on Trieste/TAL; mountain terrain and pump-station reliability; no fallback route now that BSP is dead</description>
