				<Placemark>
					<name>Druzhba - 1</name>
					<description>Status: Operational
Route: Uzhhorod (Ukraine border) → Budkovce → Šahy → Bučany junction near Bratislava → Slovnaft refinery; second branch from Bučany north-west to Czechia (Kralupy, Litvínov)
Owner/Operator: Transpetrol (Slovak state) on Slovak territory; Ukrtransnafta in Ukraine; MERO ČR for the Czech branch
Capacity: Part of a ~16.7 Mt/yr southern branch; normal southern flows ~300,000–400,000 b/d before 2026
Handles: Russian Urals for Slovakia; the Czech branch stopped taking Russian crude in March 2025 (now supplied via TAL/IKL)
Recent: Flows to Slovakia stopped 27 January 2026 after the Brody drone strike; Slovakia declared an oil emergency 18 February and released 250,000 t of reserves; flows resumed ~23 April when Ukrtransnafta lifted force majeure
Strategic role: Slovnaft&apos;s main artery — Slovakia is almost entirely dependent on this line, which is why the outage hit it harder than Hungary; the flashpoint of the Kyiv–Bratislava dispute
Risks: War exposure on Ukrainian territory; the EU exemption for Russian pipeline crude is time-limited; the Adria route is the only fallback, still being expanded</description>
					<styleUrl>#op30</styleUrl>
					<LineString>
						<tessellate>1</tessellate>
						<coordinates>
							22.28976343564294,48.61992789489562,0 20.99688532122327,48.61391615952766,0 20.01437275802945,48.3815099605818,0 18.89401760534808,48.10102925276468,0 17.18942276657055,48.12583823371172,0 17.01912076660748,48.43838894762172,0 16.98230697138875,48.82528302607331,0 16.60951564351005,49.1990484396637,0 14.32744048507515,50.26164467097204,0 13.60622674186807,50.55385837947349,0 
						</coordinates>
					</LineString>
				</Placemark>
				<Placemark>
					<name>Druzhba - 2</name>
					<description>Status: Operational
Route: Uzhhorod (Ukraine border) → Fényeslitke receiving station → MOL Danube refinery at Százhalombatta; supplies the Duna and Tisza refineries; connects to the Adria pipeline at Százhalombatta
Owner/Operator: MOL (Hungarian section) / Ukrtransnafta (Ukraine)
Capacity: Part of the ~16.7 Mt/yr southern branch; Russia supplied Hungary ~95,000 b/d via this route in 2024
Handles: Russian Urals — Hungary is one of the last two EU states still receiving Russian pipeline crude
Recent: Halted 27 January – 23 April 2026 after the Brody strike (MOL drew strategic reserves and arranged seaborne cargoes via Croatia); earlier interruptions August 2025 and March 2026 from strikes on Russian pumping stations
Strategic role: MOL Danube&apos;s primary feed and the centre of Hungary&apos;s energy politics — the outage was met with Hungarian vetoes on EU aid to Ukraine and a diesel-export ban
Risks: War-zone transit; the EU exemption is expected to end; over-reliance turns every strike into a national supply event</description>
