						</coordinates>
					</LineString>
				</Placemark>
				<Placemark id="ADDEAPC000000000001">
					<name>Eilat–Ashkelon Pipeline (EAPC, Trans-Israel)</name>
					<description>Status: Operational
Route: Eilat oil port (Gulf of Aqaba) → Arava valley → Negev → Ashkelon (Mediterranean); 254 km, 42-inch — backbone of a ~747 km network that also feeds the Haifa and Ashdod refineries
Owner/Operator: Europe Asia Pipeline Company (Israeli state-owned); operations historically under strict confidentiality
Capacity: ~30 Mt/yr; ~400,000 b/d northbound and up to ~1.2 million b/d in reverse flow (project completed 2003)
Recent: Built 1968 as a covert 50/50 Israeli–Iranian venture via Liechtenstein shells; arbitration after 1979 awarded Iran over $1bn — never paid. The 2020 UAE MED-RED land-bridge MOU was frozen by the &quot;Zero Additional Risk&quot; policy (Nov 2021); EAPC is diversifying, incl. a 260 km fibre-optic corridor
Strategic role: The only Suez-bypass land bridge under a single state&apos;s control, reversible between Red Sea and Mediterranean tanker markets
Risks: War exposure at both ends (Gaza rocket range at Ashkelon, Red Sea crisis at Eilat); environmental veto on growth — the 2014 Arava spill remains one of Israel&apos;s worst; opacity of actual flows</description>
