						</coordinates>
					</LineString>
				</Placemark>
				<Placemark id="ADDBPS00000000000001">
					<name>Baltic Pipeline System (BPS-1)</name>
					<description>Status: Operational
Route: Timan-Pechora / West Siberia / Urals-Volga networks → Yaroslavl → Kirishi (KINEF refinery) → Primorsk oil terminal, Gulf of Finland; commissioned December 2001
Owner/Operator: Transneft
Capacity: Up to ~75 Mt/yr (~1.5 million b/d system); Primorsk loads ~1 million b/d of Urals plus ~300,000 b/d of diesel
Handles: Urals — Russia&apos;s flagship export grade, since 2022 shipped largely via the &quot;shadow fleet&quot; under the G7 price cap
Recent: Ukrainian long-range drones forced the first loading suspension at Primorsk in September 2025, with renewed strikes in March and May 2026; at the peak, roughly 40% of Russia&apos;s oil export capacity was assessed offline
Strategic role: Built to bypass the Baltic transit states and concentrate exports at a Russian-controlled port — the single most important outlet for seaborne Urals
Risks: Primary target of the Ukrainian deep-strike campaign; sanctions and price-cap enforcement; Gulf of Finland winter ice; single-corridor concentration</description>
