						</coordinates>
					</LineString>
				</Placemark>
				<Placemark>
					<name>East–West Crude Oil Pipeline - Petroline</name>
					<description>Status: Operational
Route: Abqaiq processing complex (Eastern Province) → ~1,200 km west across the peninsula → Yanbu terminals and refining hub on the Red Sea; parallel 48-inch and 56-inch lines
Owner/Operator: Saudi Aramco
Capacity: ~5 million b/d nameplate (one line spent years in gas service before reconversion); reported plans for +2 million b/d; built in the early-1980s &quot;Tanker War&quot; explicitly as Hormuz insurance
Recent: Since the March 2026 Hormuz closure, the kingdom&apos;s primary export artery — Saudi Arabia held 57% of its 2025 export rate in May (UAE 82%, Iraq 8%), lifting Suez flows ~30%; after the July Houthi threat to Saudi ports, Yanbu barrels route north into SUMED — 1.8 million b/d in late July, 46% of Yanbu exports
Strategic role: The reason Saudi Arabia has options and Iraq has none — first link of the only chain (Petroline → Yanbu → SUMED → Sidi Kerir → Cape) avoiding both chokepoints
Risks: 5 million b/d ceiling versus far larger Gulf-coast production; desert pumping stations already struck by drones (2019); Yanbu now inside the Houthi threat perimeter; downstream links Aramco does not control</description>
					<styleUrl>#op30</styleUrl>
