						</coordinates>
					</LineString>
				</Placemark>
				<Placemark id="0A8D0C235F3EB7ABB22C">
					<name>Adria pipeline</name>
					<description>Status: Operational
Route: Omišalj terminal (Krk, Croatia) → Sisak, where it splits: north via Gola to MOL&apos;s Duna refinery (Százhalombatta), connecting there to the southern Druzhba and on to Slovnaft; east toward Serbia (NIS refineries); spurs to Slovenia, Bosnia and Rijeka
Owner/Operator: JANAF (Croatian state-controlled); commissioned ~1980 as the Yugoslav import artery
Capacity: The number at the heart of the fight — Croatia claims 11–15 Mt/yr toward Hungary; no more than ~2.2 Mt/yr has ever moved; Slovak section max ~3.68 Mt/yr
Recent: The 2026 Druzhba outage turned the dispute white-hot — MOL filed an EC complaint (4 March) alleging abusive tariffs; ~10-month independent capacity tests began 11 March (40,000 t/day by autumn, clarity expected early 2027), against the end-2027 cutoff of Russian supplies; a permanent expansion was decided
Strategic role: The designated non-Russian route for Hungary, Slovakia and Serbia — nominal on the map, unproven in the physics; the bottleneck is tariffs, contracts and trust as much as steel
Risks: Unproven sustained throughput; monopoly-pricing dispute in Brussels&apos; hands; Urals-configured refineries on the demand side; single terminal at Omišalj; sanctions-interpretation freezes</description>
