				<Placemark id="0B66F491DC3F2390C62D">
					<name>Caspian Pipeline Consortium</name>
					<description>Status: Operational
Route: Tengiz field (western Kazakhstan) → ~1,500 km across Kazakh and Russian territory → CPC Marine Terminal near Novorossiysk, loading offshore via SPM buoys; carries light sweet CPC Blend
Owner/Operator: Caspian Pipeline Consortium — Transneft largest holder, with KazMunayGas, Chevron, Lukoil, ExxonMobil, Rosneft-Shell, Eni and others; the consortium itself is not under Western sanctions
Capacity: ~1.5 million b/d; 1.48 million b/d average in 2026; foreign shippers moved &gt;75% of 2025&apos;s 70.5 Mt — mostly Kazakh barrels, lifted by Western majors, exiting via a Russian port
Recent: Four tankers attacked by Ukrainian drones in four days (July 2026) while loading — repeated suspensions; CPC Blend at a $2.65/bbl discount to Dated Brent; the terminal is undamaged, but vessels hesitate to berth under drones
Strategic role: ~80% of Kazakhstan&apos;s exports on one artery — the map&apos;s clearest case of collateral chokepoint risk: infrastructure targeted for its address, not its cargo
Risks: Drone and naval attack at the loading zone; war-risk premiums and berthing refusals throttling liftings without physical damage; fallbacks (BTC, Atyrau–Samara, the China line) are fractions of its size; Russian regulatory leverage</description>
