				<Placemark id="03F676C2DF3F2D848ACF">
					<name>BTC pipeline (Baku–Tbilisi–Ceyhan)</name>
					<description>Status: Operational
Route: Sangachal terminal (Baku) → Georgia via Tbilisi → Ceyhan, Türkiye; ~1,770 km, eight pump stations; Azeri Light plus Kazakh volumes shuttled Aktau→Baku by small Caspian tankers
Owner/Operator: BTC Co — operational control transferred from BP to SOCAR on 1 July 2026; BP remains a major shareholder alongside MOL, Equinor, TPAO, Eni, TotalEnergies, ExxonMobil and others
Capacity: ~1.2 million b/d nameplate, structurally underutilised as ACG production declines — that spare space is exactly what makes it the region&apos;s escape route
Recent: After the CPC strikes, Kazakhstan redirected ~300,000 t in December via BTC and other routes, targeting 1.5–2.2 Mt via BTC in 2026 (vs ~1.2 Mt in 2025); all Kazakh alternatives combined moved ~4.1 Mt against ~60 Mt/yr through CPC
Strategic role: The insurance policy that can&apos;t cover the loss — the pipe has room, the Caspian shuttle fleet doesn&apos;t; a route&apos;s capacity is its narrowest segment
Risks: Aktau port and shuttle-tanker bottleneck; commercial dependence on Azerbaijan for competing barrels; Georgia and eastern Türkiye transit (2008 sabotage precedent); Ceyhan concentration shared with the Iraq line</description>
