				<Placemark id="0C351F9C1A3F3A1EDA6E">
					<name>Southern Iraq Crude Oil Pipeline Network</name>
					<description>Status: Operational
Route: Southern giant fields (Rumaila, West Qurna 1 &amp; 2, Zubair, Majnoon) → Zubair/Fao storage depots → subsea pipelines → Al-Basra Oil Terminal (ABOT) and SPM buoys; Khor al-Amaya largely idle alongside
Owner/Operator: Basra Oil Company; exports marketed by SOMO; IOC service contracts (BP at Rumaila, Lukoil at West Qurna 2, Eni at Zubair)
Capacity: ~3.4 million b/d nominal; normally ~3.3 million b/d — 93% of Iraq&apos;s exports, funding ~90% of the state budget; grades Basrah Medium and Heavy
Recent: Seaborne exports collapsed to 260,000 b/d in May 2026 — 8% of the 2025 average; production halved to 1.9 million b/d by June; storage filled within days of the March closure, forcing field shut-ins; tankers struck near Basra; workaround — 500–700 tanker trucks/day into Syria feeding ~130,000 b/d of exports from Baniyas
Strategic role: Sole outlet for OPEC&apos;s second-largest producer, 100% Hormuz-dependent — the market&apos;s clearest proof that routes, not reserves, set supply
Risks: Hormuz as single point of failure; tanker strikes at the loading zone; Fao storage saturation; fiscal exposure; bypasses (Basra–Haditha, Basra–Aqaba) years away</description>
