				<Placemark id="0F4DDE01573FA0A395D9">
					<name>Rotterdam - Rhine Pipeline (RRP)</name>
					<description>Status: Operational
Route: Two systems, 457 km. Crude: 36-inch, 177 km from Europoort to the Venlo tank terminal (~210,000 m³), splitting north to Gelsenkirchen (Ruhr) and south to Shell Rheinland (Godorf/Wesseling). Products: 155 km from Pernis to the German border near Venlo
Owner/Operator: Rotterdam-Rijn Pijpleiding (founded 1958) — Shell, Ruhr Oel, BP
Capacity: ~25 Mt/yr — roughly 16 Mt crude + 9 Mt products, entirely underground
Recent: Its customer base is shrinking as German refining converts (Wesseling crude ended 2025); the right-of-way is the backbone of the proposed Delta Corridor (hydrogen east, CO₂ back west); the products line runs hot in the record-crack European diesel market
Strategic role: The Atlantic gateway — Rhine-Ruhr refineries price off the well-supplied Atlantic pool while TAL and SPSE customers price off the disrupted Med; VAROPreem&apos;s Rotterdam SAF plant is being built at its mouth
Risks: Demand-side decline as captive refineries convert or close; concentration on two delivery points; transition timing on the Delta Corridor; Rotterdam port dependence</description>
