				<Placemark id="0732962C843FE62A3117">
					<name>SPSE (south european pipeline)</name>
					<description>Status: Operational
Route: Fos-sur-Mer / Lavéra → Rhône and Saône valleys → St-Quentin-Fallavier (feeding Feyzin) and Gennes/Besançon, where crude passes into VARO&apos;s Jura line over the mountains to Cressier; the original main line ran 769 km to Karlsruhe, the network peaked at ~1,850 km
Owner/Operator: Société du Pipeline Sud-Européen (founded 1958 by sixteen majors); also a storage operator — 40 floating-roof tanks / 2.26 million m³ at Fos holding ~20 grades, berths discharging tankers to 400,000 t
Capacity: ~8 Mt/yr today to its two remaining customers; the Strasbourg and Lyon lines are inactive, the 34-inch runs only Fos→Besançon
Recent: Plans to convert the Lyon–Fos line to CO₂ transport, ≥4 Mt/yr from 2030
Strategic role: Cressier&apos;s lifeline — the only crude path into Switzerland; set beside Bayernoil-on-TAL, both VAROPreem landlocked refineries hang off Mediterranean tankers at opposite ends of the same sea
Risks: Two-customer concentration in its purest form — if Feyzin stops refining, Cressier alone carries the fixed costs; Fos strike habit; Mediterranean supply-pool exposure; CO₂ transition timing</description>
