				<Placemark id="006A208CDA406775DAEF">
					<name>Sumed pipeline</name>
					<description>Status: Operational
Route: Ain Sokhna terminal (Gulf of Suez) → twin 42-inch lines, ~320 km across Egypt → Sidi Kerir (Mediterranean, near Alexandria) — ~19.5 million bbl of storage in 28 tanks, SBMs serving tankers to 500,000 dwt
Owner/Operator: Arab Petroleum Pipelines Co — EGPC 50%, Saudi Aramco 15%, UAE 15%, Kuwait 15%, Qatar 5%; commissioned 1977
Capacity: ~2.5 million b/d; normal role — laden VLCCs too deep for the Canal lighten at Ain Sokhna, pump the difference overland, reload at Sidi Kerir
Recent: Flows surged after the March 2026 Hormuz closure and the July Houthi threat to Saudi ports — Yanbu crude bound for SUMED hit 1.8 million b/d in late July (+66% week-on-week, 46% of Yanbu exports); Bab el-Mandeb transits fell ~30% in a day; Asia-bound cargoes now load at Sidi Kerir and sail the Cape
Strategic role: The escape valve of 2026 — the one route that turns Saudi barrels into Mediterranean barrels without touching either chokepoint
Risks: Utilisation near the ceiling; the Yanbu→Ain Sokhna leg is itself within Houthi reach; single-country transit; Cape economics for Asian buyers; flows can collapse back as fast as they surged</description>
					<LookAt>
						<longitude>32.33991604469095</longitude>
						<latitude>29.66363795652754</latitude>
						<altitude>30.93555338953258</altitude>
