					<styleUrl>#op30</styleUrl>
					<LineString>
						<coordinates>
							29.67384348728239,31.03842348580901,0 32.34013685664577,29.6632277127853,0 
						</coordinates>
					</LineString>
				</Placemark>
				<Placemark>
					<name>TAL pipeline</name>
					<description>Status: Operational
Route: SIOT Trieste → over the Alps → Austria (AWP branch at Würmlach) → Bavaria, splitting at Ingolstadt/Vohburg: west to Karlsruhe (MiRO), east to Neustadt — the two ends of Bayernoil — with the IKL connection at Vohburg toward Czechia; 753 km, 13 pump stations
Owner/Operator: TAL Group (SIOT / TAL Österreich / Deutsche Transalpine) — OMV 32%, Shell 19%, Eni, ExxonMobil, Gunvor, Phillips 66, TotalEnergies, VAROPreem 9%, MERO 5%, Rosneft ~11% under German trusteeship
Capacity: ~43 Mt/yr design (~850,000 b/d), ~37 Mt actual; covers 100% of southern Germany&apos;s crude needs, 90% of Austria&apos;s, 100% of Czechia&apos;s
Recent: TAL-PLUS (completed April 2025, ~€44m of pump upgrades, no new pipe) raised Czech deliveries to 8 Mt/yr as Druzhba imports ended; in 2026, every barrel prices off the disrupted Mediterranean pool — freight and war-risk premiums set a thousand kilometres south
Strategic role: VAROPreem&apos;s umbilical — both Bayernoil sites sit at the eastern terminus; Europe&apos;s quiet single point of dependence
Risks: No substitute for three countries&apos; refining if Trieste stops; allocation tension as Czech volumes push utilisation toward the ceiling; Alpine transit; a trusteed Russian stake in the shareholder room</description>
					<styleUrl>#op30</styleUrl>
					<LineString>
						<tessellate>1</tessellate>
						<coordinates>
							13.76228349369942,45.64661359195608,0 13.85767312847591,45.61548374646001,0 13.26435795224233,46.07259942907043,0 12.96374937533919,46.67711969847841,0 12.11198020719152,47.60071153174676,0 11.46181430714684,48.80694359842674,0 8.336508657902893,49.0511784262862,0 
						</coordinates>
					</LineString>
				</Placemark>
				<Placemark>
					<name>Abu Dhabi Crude Oil Pipeline</name>
					<description>Status: Operational
Route: Habshan hub (Abu Dhabi, gathering the Murban system) → ~380 km east across the Hajar mountains → Fujairah terminal on the Gulf of Oman — seaward of Hormuz, inside one of the world&apos;s largest bunkering and storage hubs (incl. rock caverns)
Owner/Operator: ADNOC; commissioned 2012, explicitly as chokepoint insurance
Capacity: ~1.5 million b/d; loads Murban — the underlying of the IFAD futures contract, making Fujairah a pricing point beyond the strait
Recent: The reason the UAE outperformed every Gulf producer in 2026 — 82% of its 2025 export rate in May (Saudi 57%, Iraq 8%), plus a shuttle service to STS transfers off Oman; a second Fujairah line is planned to double capacity outside Hormuz
Strategic role: The cleanest chokepoint solution on the map — Saudi barrels need a three-link chain to escape both straits, Emirati barrels need one pipe; Fujairah&apos;s storage and futures infrastructure compounds the edge
Risks: 1.5 million b/d ceiling — everything above it still needs Hormuz; single corridor and terminal zone (Fujairah attacked 2019); congestion as the region&apos;s diverted volumes converge; the Gulf of Oman is beyond the strait but not beyond Iranian reach</description>
					<styleUrl>#op30</styleUrl>
					<LineString>
						<tessellate>1</tessellate>
						<coordinates>
							53.61652943762361,23.82677165684339,0 55.73844495787689,24.23826558587521,0 56.35414850314482,25.1987654300518,0 
						</coordinates>
					</LineString>
				</Placemark>
				<Placemark>
					<name>Trans-Sakhalin Oil Pipeline — Sakhalin-2</name>
					<description>Status: Operational
Route: Piltun-Astokhskoye and Lunskoye offshore fields → pipelines south across Sakhalin Island → Prigorodnoye complex, where gas is liquefied and crude loads by tanker
Owner/Operator: Sakhalin Energy — the Sakhalin-2 project, one of Russia&apos;s largest integrated offshore oil, gas and LNG developments
Handles: Sakhalin Blend — light, sweet, attractive to Asian refiners; produced alongside the LNG chain
Recent: Japan retains a specific US/G7 sanctions exemption for Sakhalin-related crude and LNG — Japanese utilities depend on the LNG, Mitsui and Mitsubishi hold minority stakes, and halting the crude would disrupt the whole chain
Strategic role: The sanctions carve-out of the Pacific — oil that keeps flowing because gas depends on it
Risks: Sanctions-policy change; single-complex concentration at Prigorodnoye; Far East winter operations</description>
					<styleUrl>#op30</styleUrl>
					<LineString>
						<tessellate>1</tessellate>
						<coordinates>
							144.1074789938756,51.89382377881079,0 143.0044430218798,52.13935974813346,0 142.3090114780793,47.48793494237542,0 142.9083036860729,46.63395468962762,0 
						</coordinates>
					</LineString>
				</Placemark>
				<Placemark>
					<name>OK1 pipeline</name>
					<description>Status: Operational
Route: Haoud El Hamra dispatch hub (near Hassi Messaoud) → ~645 km north across the desert and Atlas ranges → Skikda refinery and export terminal; 34-inch, in service since 1972 (sister lines: OZ1 to Arzew, OB1 to Bejaïa)
Owner/Operator: Sonatrach TRC; eight 51,200 m³ crude tanks at Haoud El Hamra; parallel NK1 condensate line to Skikda
Handles: Saharan Blend — light, sweet, ~0.1% sulphur, among the highest-quality export grades in the Mediterranean
Recent: Pump-station extension and renovation to raise capacity; in 2026, Algerian barrels are among the most logistics-advantaged in the basin — no strait, no canal, a two-day sail to Europe
Strategic role: The quiet winner of the map — field to Mediterranean water entirely within one country, in a year when the market prices routes over reserves
Risks: Ageing steel (sister OB1 needed 164 km rebuilt); single dispatch hub; mature Hassi Messaoud decline rates; desert transit security; domestic-refinery vs export allocation</description>
					<styleUrl>#op30</styleUrl>
					<LineString>
						<tessellate>1</tessellate>
						<coordinates>
							6.072725985520771,31.68076966457056,0 6.072464222170694,31.68182727170756,0 6.068266670113576,31.68531981330679,0 5.971641272361461,31.88943613148089,0 6.944895569667693,36.87937363293816,0 
						</coordinates>
					</LineString>
				</Placemark>
				<Placemark id="062176C7573EB75DB130">
					<name>Druzhba pipeline</name>
					<description>Status: Operational
Route: Almetyevsk (Tatarstan) → Samara → Bryansk → Mozyr (Belarus), splitting: north via Poland (Adamowo, Płock) to Schwedt and Leuna; south via Ukraine (Brody hub) to Slovakia (Slovnaft) and Hungary (Duna); ~5,500 km, in service since 1964 — &quot;Druzhba&quot; means &quot;friendship&quot;
Owner/Operator: Transneft on the Russian trunk; national operators per segment (Belarus, Ukrtransnafta, PERN) — every border a leverage point
Capacity: Northern branch designed for &gt;1 million b/d; southern branch low hundreds of thousands b/d
Handles: North — Kazakh-branded barrels to Germany (Poland and Germany quit Russian crude voluntarily in Jan 2023); south — Russian Urals under the EU&apos;s pipeline exemption
Recent: Its worst crisis in 2026 — the 27 January Brody strike cut southern flows for ~3 months; Slovakia declared an energy emergency, Hungary vetoed EU sanctions and a €90bn Ukraine loan until flows resumed 22 April; lasting consequence: permanent Adria expansion
Strategic role: The last Russian crude artery into the EU — simultaneously sanctions loophole, war target and veto generator
Risks: War damage with contested attribution; transit leverage at every border; the EU exemption is meant to be temporary; Urals-locked refineries; ageing Soviet steel; the 2019 contamination precedent — quality can be weaponised too</description>
