				<Placemark>
					<name>Samah/Dahra-Es Sider pipeline</name>
					<description>Status: Operational
Route: Samah and Dahra fields (Sirte Basin desert) → 32-inch line north → Es Sider terminal, Libya&apos;s largest oil port (~350,000 b/d export capacity); loads the flagship Es Sider grade
Owner/Operator: Waha Oil Company — Libyan NOC 59.2%, ConocoPhillips 16.3%, TotalEnergies 16.3%, Hess 8.2% — one of the last places US majors lift Libyan equity crude
Capacity: The Waha fields can pump ~300,000 b/d (recently ~285,000); the line, not the fields, sets the ceiling — shutdowns have cut national output by ~200,000 b/d at a stroke
Recent: NOC describes the line as dilapidated — leaks, no maintenance budget, blockades (2020, 2022), emergency repair shutdowns (2021, 2022); in 2026 every outage tightens the Med light sweet pool Europe leans on
Strategic role: An onshore chokepoint — one corroded 32-inch pipe in the desert moves a fifth of a country&apos;s production
Risks: Mechanical failure as the base case; militia and PFG action at both ends; force-majeure frequency; underinvestment locked in by fiscal chaos; the Gulf of Sirte cluster can be blockaded as a unit</description>
