			<Placemark>
				<name>RMR</name>
				<description>Status: Operational
Route: Venlo (Dutch border) → Dinslaken → Cologne-Godorf/Wesseling → through the Eifel and Hunsrück → fork at the Udenheim pump station: (a) Gustavsburg/Flörsheim/Raunheim for Frankfurt and its airport, (b) Ludwigshafen/BASF Oppau; 525 km, fully underground, nine pump stations
Owner/Operator: RMR GmbH, Cologne-Godorf (founded 1965); shareholders Shell, BP, Esso/ExxonMobil
Capacity: ~15 million m³ (~12 Mt) of products a year — some 400,000 road tankers
Handles: Diesel, gasoline, heating oil, jet and petrochemical feedstocks in sequenced batches; fed at Venlo by the RRP products line, topped up at Cologne by Godorf/Wesseling
Recent: €100m+ modernisation with a new Godorf control centre (2025); its supply-security role grows as German crude runs decline (Wesseling shutdown); the line ends at Frankfurt/Ludwigshafen — it does NOT reach Karlsruhe, Strasbourg or Basel
Strategic role: The counter-example to the Rhine chokepoint — pipeline-fed Frankfurt Airport and BASF held up through 2018, 2022 and 2026 while barge-only Strasbourg and Basel paid record freight; the boundary runs at Ludwigshafen
Risks: Single-system dependence for Rhine-Main jet fuel; 1991 Dinslaken rupture precedent; no physical extension south despite rising strategic weight</description>
