				</Placemark>
				<Placemark>
					<name>Saharan Blend route</name>
					<description>Status: Operational
Crude: Saharan Blend — a commingled export blend, not a single field
API: ~45°
Sulfur: ~0.1%
Type: Light, sweet
Origin: Algeria — blended from Saharan fields (dominated by Hassi Messaoud, gathered at Haoud El Hamra), exported through Skikda, Arzew or Bejaïa
Markets: Mainly the Mediterranean and NW Europe; additional sales to the Americas
Used for: High light-distillate yield — naphtha, gasoline, jet, diesel — plus petrochemical feed; minimal desulphurisation cost, ideal for simpler and mid-complexity refineries
Pricing: Monthly Sonatrach OSP as a differential to Dated Brent, frequently at a premium
Vessels: Usually Aframax; Suezmax for larger cargoes
Transit: ~2 days / ~450 nm from Skikda to Fos-sur-Mer; ~5–6 days to Rotterdam via Gibraltar
Risks: Low — no major chokepoints; Gulf of Lion mistral weather, port congestion, occasional Algerian loading or pipeline disruptions</description>
					<styleUrl>#stylemap_id020</styleUrl>
					<LineString>
						<tessellate>1</tessellate>
						<coordinates>
							6.939508765107738,36.88880302234791,0 6.597759024239593,37.60688030029786,0 5.79435056388273,38.80280264287649,0 5.192549632927312,40.50242277568254,0 4.938327436476548,43.38064883167617,0 4.901126363949658,43.41450603818715,0 
						</coordinates>
					</LineString>
				</Placemark>
				<Placemark>
					<name>Es Sider route</name>
					<description>Status: Operational
Crude: Es Sider — a commercial export blend named after its terminal, fed by several Sirte Basin fields
API: ~37°
Sulfur: ~0.4%
Type: Light, sweet
Origin: Libya — gathered from Waha, Gialo and other Sirte Basin fields, exported at Es Sider via offshore SBMs
Markets: Mainly the Mediterranean and NW Europe (Italy, Spain, France, Germany, Netherlands); occasional Asia sales
Used for: High gasoline and middle-distillate yield with low sulphur — suits Mediterranean and mid-complexity refineries
Pricing: Monthly NOC/Waha OSP as a differential to Dated Brent, with a Libyan political-risk element
Vessels: Usually Suezmax or Aframax, loaded offshore at the SBMs
Transit: ~5–6 days / ~1,300–1,450 nm to Fos-sur-Mer via the Strait of Sicily; ~7–9 days to Rotterdam via Gibraltar
Risks: Libyan political instability — field shutdowns, port blockades and force majeure at the terminal; militia risk; Strait of Sicily traffic</description>
					<styleUrl>#stylemap_id25</styleUrl>
					<LineString>
						<tessellate>1</tessellate>
						<coordinates>
							18.35596912396328,30.68834646924192,0 13.54114224798351,35.22509704572018,0 13.3339340684015,36.43726026793413,0 8.117250326889776,38.11726405364553,0 4.935933745798977,43.42443173647109,0 
						</coordinates>
					</LineString>
				</Placemark>
				<Placemark>
					<name>Girassol route</name>
					<description>Status: Operational
Crude: Girassol Blend — the commingled Girassol + Jasmim + Rosa stream processed on the Girassol FPSO
API: ~30°
Sulfur: ~0.34%
Type: Medium, sweet
Origin: Angola, deepwater Block 17 — exported directly offshore via a CALM buoy; no onshore terminal or pipeline
Markets: Mainly China (over half of Angolan exports) and India; material share to Europe (Spain, Netherlands)
Used for: Good gasoil and middle-distillate yield with low sulphur — suits complex NW European and Asian cracking refineries
Pricing: Variable differential to Dated Brent, in line with West African OSPs
Vessels: Suezmax to Europe; VLCC for Asia — vessel size drives the discharge port via draft limits
Transit: ~20–24 days / ~6,400–6,900 nm up the Atlantic to Lysekil; longer to Asia via the Cape
Risks: Gulf of Guinea piracy at loading; Atlantic and North Sea weather; VLCC draft limits. No Suez, Hormuz or Gibraltar transit</description>
					<styleUrl>#stylemap_id240</styleUrl>
					<LineString>
						<tessellate>1</tessellate>
