				<name>Rivers &amp; canals</name>
				<Placemark>
					<name>Rhin River</name>
					<description>Status: Reduced
Route: Rotterdam (Europoort) → Duisburg → Cologne → Koblenz → Kaub → Mainz → Ludwigshafen/Mannheim → Karlsruhe → Strasbourg → Basel; ~800 km of navigable Rhine
Handles: Diesel, gasoline, heating oil, jet and chemical feedstocks moving inland from ARA refineries and storage; coal, grains, ores, containers
Connectivity: Feeds inland depots and refineries — Strasbourg&apos;s Rhine port and the Basel gateway supplying Switzerland and Cressier
Recent: Record low water in August 2026 — Kaub at ~21 cm on 3–4 Aug (lowest since records began in 1880), under 10 cm on 14 Aug; barges cut to about a third of normal cargo (~460 t vs ~1,200 t through Kaub); ARA→south Germany product freight up ~400% in two months; ~60 trucks to replace one 1,500 t barge; no return to normal expected before October
Strategic role: The physical link between the ARA pricing hub and inland demand — when the river drops, inland prices decouple from Rotterdam (2018: Rotterdam–Basel transport cost went from ~$5/bbl to over $35/bbl)
Risks: Recurring summer droughts (2018, 2022, 2026); Kaub as a single shallow chokepoint; limited pipeline and rail substitution for Switzerland and Alsace</description>
					<styleUrl>#msn_ylw-pushpin01</styleUrl>
					<LineString>
