				<Placemark>
					<name>Lower Mississippi Deep-Draft Corridor</name>
					<description>Status: Operational
Route: Baton Rouge — the head of deep-draft navigation, where ocean tankers end 380 km inland — past the St. James hub and the Garyville, Norco and St. Charles refineries, through New Orleans, to the Gulf via Southwest Pass
Owner/Operator: US Army Corps of Engineers (channel); pilots&apos; associations govern transit
Handles: Crude in by tanker to the refining row; products, chemicals and grain out; above Baton Rouge the river continues as the barge system feeding the Midwest — America&apos;s Rhine, complete with its own low-water crises (2022–23 barge restrictions)
Recent: The 2022–23 drought forced draft and tow-size restrictions on the barge system — the American echo of Kaub, moving freight rates the same way
Strategic role: The reason the corridor exists — deep water, barge connectivity, cooling water and pipeline convergence on one river; its chokepoint is Southwest Pass, the single dredged mouth all deep-draft traffic transits
Risks: Dredging and sediment economics; hurricane closures; drought restricting the barge leg; a Southwest Pass blockage would idle the row</description>
