				<Placemark>
					<name>Mersin Karaduvar Fuel Terminals Cluster</name>
					<description>Status: Operational
Owner/Operator: Multiple independents — Opet (240,000 m³, pier, mooring buoy, rail and customs transit tanks), Kadoil Akdeniz (141,000 m³, offshore platform berthing two 65,000 dwt vessels), Soil Petrol (96,500 m³) and others alongside ATAŞ
Role: Turkey&apos;s Mediterranean products-import cluster — seaborne gasoline, diesel and fuel oil landed, stored, blended and distributed to southern and central Anatolia by truck and rail
Connectivity: No pipeline serves this coast — when the ATAŞ refinery closed in 2004, the region&apos;s fuel had to come 485 km by road from Kırıkkale, and the logistics haven&apos;t fundamentally changed
Strategic role: The demand-side mirror of Ceyhan next door — one bay exports crude the country doesn&apos;t refine, the other imports products the country doesn&apos;t produce
Risks: Full seaborne dependence; road-distribution bottlenecks; transit-trade compliance exposure</description>
					<styleUrl>#msn_red-circle40</styleUrl>
					<Point>
						<coordinates>34.68769017933675,36.82386007525118,0</coordinates>
					</Point>
				</Placemark>
				<Placemark>
					<name>ATAŞ Terminal Mersin (former refinery)</name>
					<description>Status: Operational
Owner/Operator: ATAŞ — Petrol Ofisi (Vitol) 61.45%, Shell &amp; Turcas 38.55%; Turcas sold its direct stake in March 2026
Role: The Mediterranean coast&apos;s products hub — a 1962 refinery (Mobil/Shell/BP/Caltex heritage, 3.2 Mt/yr) that ceased refining in June 2004 and was converted into one of Turkey&apos;s largest fuel terminals
Storage capacity: 577,000 m³ of gasoline, diesel, fuel oil and biofuel components; jetty for large product tankers; rail and truck loading
Recent: August 2026 — the MR tanker Wendrix loaded gasoline here bound for Russia, the first identified Turkey-to-Russia seaborne gasoline route of the sanctions era; Mersin port sits on Ukraine&apos;s sanctions list over suspected Russian links
Strategic role: A trader-owned Mediterranean blending and re-export platform — where stored molecules change direction when the market flips, as Russia&apos;s 2026 switch from fuel exporter to importer demonstrated
Risks: Sanctions-compliance exposure on re-export flows; earthquake vulnerability of the tank farm; single-coast products dependence for the region since refining ended</description>
					<styleUrl>#msn_red-circle6</styleUrl>
					<Point>
						<coordinates>34.70087210231715,36.8193020811484,0</coordinates>
					</Point>
				</Placemark>
				<Placemark>
					<name>Novi Sad NIS site</name>
					<description>Status: Operational
Owner/Operator: NIS (Naftna Industrija Srbije)
Role: Former crude refinery, no longer processing fuels — now NIS&apos;s head office, a lubricants operation and a Danube logistics base
Recent: Heavily damaged in the 1999 NATO bombing and never returned to full crude refining; NIS concentrated all refining at Pančevo
Strategic role: A reminder that Serbia went from two refineries to one — why the Pančevo sanctions crisis had no domestic fallback</description>
