				<Placemark>
					<name>Strasbourg oil terminal</name>
					<description>Status: Operational
Owner/Operator: Seven Seveso high-threshold sites run by independent storage companies (incl. Bolloré Energy, Tepsa) within the Port Autonome de Strasbourg
Role: Inland refined-products storage and distribution hub — France&apos;s second river port; diesel, gasoline, heating oil, jet — no crude; dedicated oil basin (1920s, rebuilt post-war) with barge berths, rail and truck loading
Connectivity: Supplied mainly by barge from the ARA hub via the Rhine through Kaub; distributes across Alsace and eastern France
Recent: Directly exposed to the August 2026 record low water — reduced barge loads and a ~400% freight surge tighten and reprice inland supply
Strategic role: The end-point of the ARA-to-Alsace product chain — where a Rhine low-water event becomes a local fuel-supply and price issue
Risks: Rhine navigability (2018, 2022, 2026); Seveso sites 100 m from housing (contested risk plan); limited pipeline alternatives for the region</description>
					<LookAt>
						<longitude>7.810384275813795</longitude>
						<latitude>48.59896112534291</latitude>
						<altitude>0</altitude>
						<heading>0.03761259590973835</heading>
						<tilt>12.33929695322486</tilt>
						<range>3728.000555799524</range>
						<gx:altitudeMode>relativeToSeaFloor</gx:altitudeMode>
					</LookAt>
					<styleUrl>#default00</styleUrl>
					<Point>
						<gx:drawOrder>1</gx:drawOrder>
						<coordinates>7.803447355785695,48.60168919049215,0</coordinates>
					</Point>
				</Placemark>
				<Placemark>
					<name>Birsfelden Tank Farms</name>
					<description>Status: Operational
Owner/Operator: Port owned by the canton of Basel-Landschaft, managed by Schweizerische Rheinhäfen; tank farms privately run — VARO operates two fuel tank farms here; Birsterminal AG runs the largest cargo facility in the Swiss Rhine ports
Role: Northern half of Switzerland&apos;s barge-fed fuel gateway — diesel, gasoline, heating oil and jet landed by Rhine barge; also containers, steel and general cargo
Connectivity: End-point of the ARA–Basel corridor through Kaub; rail and truck distribution into the Swiss network
Recent: Designated site of the H2-Hub Schweiz — announced 2021 (ports, VARO, AVIA, IWB), association founded Feb 2024; IWB pilot green-hydrogen plant planned from 2026/27, European-network imports early 2030s
Strategic role: With Muttenz, the physical entry point of Switzerland&apos;s seaborne fuel supply — where the Rhine corridor from ARA ends and Swiss inland distribution begins
Risks: Total dependence on Rhine navigability (2018, 2022, record 2026); landlocked market with limited alternatives (Fos–Cressier pipeline and rail only)</description>
					<LookAt>
						<longitude>7.636677907105755</longitude>
						<latitude>47.55498926526166</latitude>
						<altitude>0</altitude>
						<heading>0.006970041236957261</heading>
						<tilt>3.170333229084024</tilt>
						<range>1840.506306391863</range>
						<gx:altitudeMode>relativeToSeaFloor</gx:altitudeMode>
					</LookAt>
					<styleUrl>#default00</styleUrl>
					<Point>
						<gx:drawOrder>1</gx:drawOrder>
						<coordinates>7.637289037624486,47.55480931641503,0</coordinates>
					</Point>
				</Placemark>
				<Placemark>
					<name>Muttenz Tank Farms</name>
					<description>Status: Operational
Owner/Operator: Port managed by Schweizerische Rheinhäfen; principal operators TAU Tanklager Auhafen (134 tanks, ~530,000 m³, up to seven barges discharging simultaneously, rail and truck loading) and AVIA (grouping ten independent Swiss firms)
Role: Switzerland&apos;s main liquid-fuel import and storage terminal — the largest tank-farm concentration in the Swiss Rhine ports; also edible oils, grains and dry bulk
Connectivity: Delivered almost entirely by Rhine barge from ARA via Kaub; onward by rail and truck across Switzerland; designated storage site for compulsory Swiss fuel stocks
Recent: Built 1937–40 and repeatedly expanded; Basel-area tank capacity consolidating toward Auhafen; part of the H2-Hub Schweiz initiative; directly hit by the August 2026 record low water, with ARA–Upper Rhine freight near €200/tonne
Strategic role: The single most important node of Switzerland&apos;s fuel import chain — when barge freight surges, Swiss inland prices decouple from Rotterdam
Risks: Rhine low water; Seveso-class constraints in a dense urban area; national fuel imports concentrated at one complex</description>
