			<Folder>
				<name>Strategic / emergency reserves</name>
				<Placemark>
					<name>Bayou Choctaw Strategic Petroleum Reserve</name>
					<description>Status: Operational
Owner/Operator: US Department of Energy
Role: One of the four sites of the US Strategic Petroleum Reserve — crude stored in salt-dome caverns ~20 km southwest of Baton Rouge, within pumping distance of the river refining row
Storage capacity: ~76 million barrels of the SPR&apos;s ~714 million authorised; the national reserve held ~370 million entering 2026 after the 2021–23 drawdowns, under a mandated refill programme buying below $79
Connectivity: Pipeline ties into the St. James hub and the Capline corridor — release barrels reach refineries in days
Recent: The 2022 release — the largest in SPR history — moved through sites like this one; the 2026 price spike reopened the release-vs-refill debate with the reserve at half strength
Strategic role: The emergency barrel next door to the refineries that would need it — geography chosen precisely for that
Risks: Cavern maintenance backlog; refill economics above the price cap; the policy whiplash of using reserves as market policy</description>
