				<visibility>0</visibility>
				<Placemark>
					<name>Rompetrol Petrochemicals Năvodari</name>
					<visibility>0</visibility>
					<description>Status: Operational
Owner: Rompetrol Rafinare (KMG International), on the Petromidia platform
Configuration: Romania&apos;s only petrochemical producer — polypropylene and low-density polyethylene lines fed directly by the refinery next door
Main feedstocks: Propylene and other streams from Petromidia — the classic platform integration
Main products: PP and LDPE polymers for Romanian and regional converters
Markets: Romania and southeast Europe — the domestic plastics industry&apos;s sole local source
Strategic role: The demand-side of the Năvodari platform — the reason Petromidia&apos;s naphtha and LPG have a home on site; a national monopoly by default, since every competitor closed
Risks: Single-site national dependence for polymers; petrochemical margin cycles; platform interdependence with the refinery&apos;s turnarounds</description>
					<styleUrl>#msn_icon?color=000000&amp;id=2000&amp;scale=4</styleUrl>
					<Point>
						<coordinates>28.6431447951377,44.34213864754286,0</coordinates>
					</Point>
				</Placemark>
				<Placemark>
					<name>LyondellBasell Wesseling</name>
					<visibility>0</visibility>
					<description>Status: Operational
Owner/Operator: LyondellBasell (plants operated with Basell Polyolefine); in operation since 1953, 2.7 km², own 470 MW power plant; Knapsack sub-site ~10 miles away
Configuration: Two steam crackers (~1.04 Mt/yr ethylene combined), four HDPE, two LDPE and three PP plants plus an advanced-polyolefins unit — &gt;2.2 Mt/yr of polyolefins
Main feedstocks: Naphtha by pipeline from the neighbouring Shell Rheinland refineries plus external imports; trials of Neste bio-based hydrocarbons for partly bio-based PE/PP
Connectivity: Pipeline-linked to Godorf/Wesseling and Dormagen; olefin lines toward Ludwigshafen, Antwerp and Chemelot; Rhine barge access
Recent: The end of Shell Wesseling crude distillation (2025) removed a local naphtha source — the cluster&apos;s three crackers need ~3 Mt/yr; LyondellBasell is building its first commercial-scale MoReTec chemical-recycling plant here; 2024 European footprint review amid ethylene overcapacity
Strategic role: The demand-side half of the Cologne cluster — the reason the Rhineland naphtha balance matters
Risks: Naphtha supply as regional refining shrinks; European overcapacity; Rhine low water; energy-cost gap versus US and Middle East crackers</description>
