		<Folder>
			<name>Chokepoints</name>
			<visibility>0</visibility>
			<Folder>
				<name>Straits &amp; canals</name>
				<visibility>0</visibility>
				<Placemark>
					<name>Strait of Hormuz</name>
					<visibility>0</visibility>
					<description>Status: Offline
Role: The world&apos;s most important oil chokepoint — the only sea passage out of the Persian Gulf; ~21 million b/d (~20% of global petroleum liquids) and ~20% of LNG in normal times; 39 km wide at the narrowest, with 3.2 km shipping lanes each way
Handles: Crude, products and LNG of Saudi Arabia, Iraq, Kuwait, UAE, Qatar and Iran — overwhelmingly toward Asia
Alternatives: Petroline to Yanbu (Saudi), ADCOP to Fujairah (UAE), STS transfers off Oman — Iraq, Kuwait and Qatar have none
Recent: Effectively closed since 28 February 2026 (Iran war) — transits ~10% of pre-war levels; the June MOU collapsed in July; escorted convoys, transit fees demanded up to $2m, thousands of vessels backlogged at the peak; VLCC Gulf–China earnings above $520,000/day and Brent near $94 (Aug 2026)
Strategic role: The reason the whole 2026 map looks the way it does — every corridor, bypass and freight rate on it reprices off this strait
Risks: Mines, drones, seizures; no reopening timeline; months of clearance work even after a deal</description>
					<styleUrl>#m_ylw-pushpin0</styleUrl>
					<Point>
						<coordinates>56.45,26.6,0</coordinates>
					</Point>
				</Placemark>
				<Placemark>
					<name>Strait of Malacca &amp; Singapore Strait</name>
					<visibility>0</visibility>
					<description>Status: Operational
Role: The main artery between the Indian Ocean and East Asia — Malacca and the Singapore Strait/Phillips Channel form one continuous chokepoint, ~2.8 km wide at its tightest; ~24 million b/d in normal times, the largest oil flow of any strait — ahead even of Hormuz
Handles: Middle Eastern and African crude eastbound to China, Japan and Korea; products, fuel oil and bunkers into the Singapore hub
Vessels: Draft-limited &quot;Malaccamax&quot; — the deepest-laden VLCCs must detour via Lombok or Sunda
Alternatives: Lombok/Sunda (+3 days); the ESPO/Kozmino route avoids it entirely from the north
Recent: 2026 — absorbing rerouted and record Asia-bound traffic through the Hormuz crisis; chronic congestion and piracy cycles
Strategic role: Singapore&apos;s raison d&apos;être — the strait made the trading, refining and bunkering hub
Risks: Extreme traffic density and collision risk; armed robbery; any closure reroutes the world&apos;s largest oil flow</description>
					<styleUrl>#m_ylw-pushpin0</styleUrl>
					<Point>
						<coordinates>101.85,2.2,0</coordinates>
					</Point>
				</Placemark>
				<Placemark>
					<name>Bab el-Mandeb Strait</name>
					<visibility>0</visibility>
					<description>Status: Reduced
Role: The gate between the Red Sea and the Gulf of Aden — southern door of the entire Suez/SUMED corridor; ~29 km wide; ~8.7 million b/d of oil and products pre-crisis
Handles: Gulf-to-Europe crude and products, Asia-bound Russian barrels, and all Suez-routed trade
Recent: Houthi attacks since late 2023 collapsed transits by ~70%; on 20 July 2026 the Houthis declared a naval blockade of Saudi Arabia — no tracked Saudi oil transits by mid-August; major Chinese shippers avoiding the strait; Yanbu cargoes shifting north into SUMED and the Jeddah land bridge instead
Strategic role: The chokepoint that turned the Cape of Good Hope into a main road — and pushed the Port of Eilat into insolvency (both visible on this map)
Risks: Missile, drone and unmanned-boat attacks; war-risk premiums; its simultaneous squeeze with Hormuz is the 2026 nightmare realised</description>
					<styleUrl>#m_ylw-pushpin0</styleUrl>
					<Point>
						<coordinates>43.33,12.58,0</coordinates>
					</Point>
				</Placemark>
				<Placemark>
					<name>Suez Canal</name>
					<visibility>0</visibility>
					<description>Status: Reduced
Role: The 193 km lock-free canal between the Mediterranean and the Red Sea — in normal times ~10% of world seaborne trade; with the parallel SUMED pipeline, ~5–9 million b/d of oil moves through the Egyptian corridor
Handles: Laden tankers northbound, containers and dry cargo both ways; VLCCs too deep for full transit lighten into SUMED at Ain Sokhna
Recent: Traffic roughly halved since the Red Sea crisis began (2023); in 2026 the two directions decoupled — northbound crude via Egypt rose ~30% after Hormuz closed (Saudi barrels reloading at Sidi Kerir), while Asia–Europe trade routes via the Cape; the 2021 Ever Given grounding remains the single-point-failure textbook case
Strategic role: Works as one system with SUMED — Egypt&apos;s twin toll booths on the Europe–Asia energy road
Risks: Bab el-Mandeb exposure at its southern approach; blockage precedent; canal-revenue collapse pressuring Egypt</description>
					<styleUrl>#m_ylw-pushpin0</styleUrl>
					<Point>
						<coordinates>32.31,30.45,0</coordinates>
					</Point>
				</Placemark>
				<Placemark>
					<name>Turkish Straits (Bosphorus &amp; Dardanelles)</name>
					<visibility>0</visibility>
					<description>Status: Operational
Role: The only exit from the Black Sea — the Bosphorus narrows to ~700 m through a city of 16 million, followed by the Dardanelles; ~3 million b/d of crude and products in normal times
Handles: Russian Urals and products from Novorossiysk and Tuapse, CPC Blend carrying Kazakh exports, Romanian, Bulgarian and Ukrainian trade
Recent: Carrying the CPC and Novorossiysk flows this map&apos;s Black Sea assets depend on; shadow-fleet tankers face mounting insurance documentation checks; the Montreux Convention has limited warship transits since 2022
Strategic role: The pinch where Kazakh oil, Russian exports and NATO geography share seven hundred metres of water
Risks: Collisions and groundings in an urban strait; fog and current closures; insurance-check delays; Black Sea escalation spilling into transit rules</description>
					<styleUrl>#m_ylw-pushpin0</styleUrl>
					<Point>
						<coordinates>29.06,41.12,0</coordinates>
					</Point>
				</Placemark>
				<Placemark>
					<name>Danish Straits (Great Belt &amp; Øresund)</name>
					<visibility>0</visibility>
					<description>Status: Operational
Role: The Baltic Sea&apos;s exit to the world — shallow, winding passages carrying ~3 million b/d of Russian crude and products from Primorsk and Ust-Luga (both on this map) plus all Baltic trade
Handles: Urals and diesel westbound largely on the &quot;shadow fleet&quot;; regional products, LNG and dry cargo
Recent: The front line of price-cap enforcement — Danish and EU inspection and insurance-verification regimes tightened through 2025–26 under successive sanctions packages; uninsured tankers anchored in the straits are a standing environmental fear
Strategic role: Where the G7 price cap is enforced — or evaded; the western mirror of Kozmino in Russia&apos;s export system
Risks: Navigation hazards in shallow water; winter ice; a major uninsured-tanker spill; escalation over inspections</description>
					<styleUrl>#m_ylw-pushpin0</styleUrl>
					<Point>
						<coordinates>11.03,55.35,0</coordinates>
					</Point>
				</Placemark>
				<Placemark>
					<name>Panama Canal</name>
					<visibility>0</visibility>
					<description>Status: Operational
Role: The lock-based shortcut between the Pacific and the Atlantic — ~5% of world seaborne trade; oil products, LPG, LNG, grain and containers; Neopanamax locks since 2016
Handles: US Gulf product and LPG exports toward Asia and the Pacific; the representative route of Canadian Cold Lake barrels toward Europe on this map
Recent: The 2023–24 drought cut transits by about a third and imposed draft limits — the world&apos;s first climate-driven chokepoint squeeze; operations and watershed management since restored and reformed
Strategic role: The LPG road from Mont Belvieu to Asia, and the freight variable in every US Gulf arbitrage eastward
Risks: Drought recurrence; lock outages; auctioned transit slots repricing freight at short notice</description>
					<styleUrl>#m_ylw-pushpin0</styleUrl>
					<Point>
						<coordinates>-79.68000000000001,9.1,0</coordinates>
					</Point>
				</Placemark>
				<Placemark>
					<name>Cape of Good Hope Route</name>
					<visibility>0</visibility>
					<description>Status: Operational
Role: The open-ocean bypass — no canal, no strait, no toll, no capacity limit: the default route whenever Suez/Bab el-Mandeb or Hormuz economics fail; adds ~10–14 days between Europe and Asia
Handles: Diverted container trade, crude and products in both directions — including Sidi Kerir loadings sailing the long way to Asia in 2026
Recent: The great beneficiary of 2024–26 — Red Sea diversions, then Hormuz-era reroutings, pushed record tonnage around Africa; Saldanha Bay and Durban (both on this map) service and monitor the flow
Strategic role: The world&apos;s insurance policy — its cost is measured in time, bunkers and working capital rather than risk premiums
Risks: Southern Ocean weather; bunker-price exposure; South African port congestion and bunkering capacity</description>
					<styleUrl>#m_ylw-pushpin0</styleUrl>
					<Point>
						<coordinates>18.3,-34.75,0</coordinates>
					</Point>
				</Placemark>
			</Folder>
		</Folder>
	</Folder>
